OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026", "title": "Uniform Bi-Holder Transport from Weak MTW", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Uniform-Bi-Holder-Transport-from-Weak-MTW-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "360", "family_title": "Weak MTW curvature gives convexity and regular optimal transport", "abstract_html": "We prove that the weak Ma–Trudinger–Wang condition on a fixed smooth connected compact boundaryless Riemannian manifold of dimension at least two implies a common Hölder estimate for optimal transport maps and their inverses over the entire class of probability densities with fixed positive upper and lower bounds. The maps have homeomorphic representatives, conjugate cut points are allowed, and no density regularity is assumed.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Uniform bi-Hölder transport — original Theorem 1.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform bi-Hölder transport — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M,g)\\) be a smooth connected compact Riemannian manifold without boundary, of dimension \\(n\\geq 2\\). Write \\(d\\) and \\(\\mathrm{vol}\\) for its distance and volume measure.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  c(x,y)=\\frac12d(x,y)^2,\\qquad\n  I(x)=\\left\\{p\\in T_xM:\n  d(x,\\exp_x(ap))=a|p|\\text{ for some }a&gt;1\\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume weak MTW: for every \\(p\\in I(x)\\) and every \\(\\xi,\\eta\\in T_xM\\) with \\(\\xi\\perp\\eta\\),\n  \\[\n  -\\frac32\n  \\left.\n  \\frac{\\partial^4}{\\partial s^2\\partial t^2}\n  c\\bigl(\\exp_x(t\\xi),\\exp_x(p+s\\eta)\\bigr)\n  \\right|_{s=t=0}\\geq0.\n  \\tag{1.1}\n  \\]\n  The cost is smooth near the pairs used in this formula; no curvature inequality at a nonsmooth cost pair is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\lambda\\leq\\Lambda&lt;\\infty\\) such that the class of probability densities bounded between \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\Lambda\\) is nonempty.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1]\\) and \\(C&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on \\((M,g),\\lambda,\\Lambda\\), such that, for every pair of measurable densities satisfying\n\\[\n\\int_M\\rho_i\\,d\\mathrm{vol}=1,\\qquad\n\\lambda\\leq\\rho_i\\leq\\Lambda\n\\quad\\mathrm{vol}\\text{-almost everywhere},\\qquad i=0,1,\n\\]\nthe almost-everywhere unique optimal map for \\(c\\) from \\(\\rho_0\\mathrm{vol}\\) to \\(\\rho_1\\mathrm{vol}\\) has a homeomorphic representative \\(\\widetilde T:M\\to M\\), and\n\\[\nd(\\widetilde T(x),\\widetilde T(x'))\n \\leq C d(x,x')^\\alpha,\n\\qquad\nd(\\widetilde T^{-1}(y),\\widetilde T^{-1}(y'))\n \\leq C d(y,y')^\\alpha\n\\]\nfor all \\(x,x',y,y'\\in M\\).</p><p>The constants are common to the full density class on the fixed manifold. No density regularity, nonfocality, strict MTW, or cut avoidance is assumed. Conjugate cut points are allowed. No explicit exponent or uniformity over varying metrics is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>McCann's polar factorization theorem supplies the almost-everywhere unique optimal map and dual potentials for each admissible density pair.</p><p>Proposition 14 applies to the supremum over the entire fixed density class, and therefore gives common \\(C_0,\\beta,r_0\\) with\n\\[\nF(r)\\leq C_0r^\\beta\\qquad(0&lt;r&lt;r_0).\n\\]\nProposition 4 gives a common Hölder estimate with\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\frac12\\min\\{\\beta,1\\}.\n\\]\nEvery pole has one contact endpoint. Applying the same result to the reversed density pair gives singleton contacts on the other side with the same constants. The two contact maps are continuous inverses and agree almost everywhere with the optimal maps.</p><p>Enlarge the Hölder constant to cover distances outside the small-scale range. This proves the uniform bi-Hölder estimate.</p><p>The exponent is obtained by contradiction and compactness; no explicit exponent or estimate uniform over varying metrics or density bounds is produced. The inverse map comes from reversal of the positive-density pair, not from time-one injectivity for an arbitrary potential.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Density-free geometric interface — original Lemmas 2.1–2.4, Theorem 2.5, Corollaries 2.6–2.7, and Proposition 2.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Density-free geometric interface — original Lemmas 2.1–2.4, Theorem 2.5, Corollaries 2.6–2.7, and Proposition 2.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the manifold, cost, and weak-MTW assumption of Theorem 1; no densities are involved.</p><p class=\"li\">A potential is a function\n  \\[\n  u(x)=\\max_{y\\in M}\\{-c(x,y)-w(y)\\}\n  \\]\n  for a continuous datum \\(w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A dual pair satisfies\n  \\[\n  u(x)=\\sup_y\\{-c(x,y)-v(y)\\},\\qquad\n  v(y)=\\sup_x\\{-c(x,y)-u(x)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_g=\\operatorname{diam}M\\), identify covectors and tangent vectors by the metric, and write \\(\\partial u\\) for the ordinary subdifferential of a semiconvex function.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  G_x=\\{p\\in T_xM:d(x,\\exp_xp)=|p|\\}=\\overline{I(x)},\n  \\quad\n  c_t=c/t,\n  \\quad\n  Q_tf(z)=\\min_x\\{f(x)+c_t(x,z)\\}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(p\\in I(x)\\), set\n  \\[\n  A_x(p)=D^2_{xx}c(x,\\exp_xp),\\qquad\n  \\sigma_x(p)=|\\det(d\\exp_x)_p|.\n  \\]\n  The function \\(\\sigma_x\\) extends continuously to \\(G_x\\). At a nonconjugate minimizing cut vector, \\(A_x\\) may denote the Hessian of the specified smooth upper cost branch.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following interface holds.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Uniform supports and active logs — original Lemma 2.1.**  \n   The cost is uniformly semiconcave in either variable, including at cut points. Every potential is \\(D_g\\)-Lipschitz and uniformly semiconvex. For\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V_u(x)=\n   \\{p\\in G_x:u(x)+c(x,\\exp_xp)+w(\\exp_xp)=0\\},\n   \\]\n   the set \\(\\mathcal V_u(x)\\) is compact and\n   \\[\n   \\partial u(x)=\\operatorname{conv}\\mathcal V_u(x).\n   \\]\n   Every \\(p\\in\\partial u(x)\\) has a positive representation by at most \\(n+1\\) affinely independent active logs. Every minimizing log to each endpoint active for \\(w\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_u(x)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Radial divided action — original Lemma 2.2.**  \n   For \\(p\\in G_x\\), let \\(S_p(r)\\) be the Jacobi tensor along \\(\\exp_x(rp)\\), in parallel orthonormal frames, with\n   \\[\n   S_p(0)=0,\\qquad D_rS_p(0)=\\mathrm{Id}.\n   \\]\n   Write \\(H_r(p)=A_x(rp)/r\\). For \\(0&lt;t&lt;s&lt;1\\),\n   \\[\n   \\frac{d}{dr}H_r(p)\n   =-S_p(r)^{-1}S_p(r)^{-T},\n   \\qquad\n   H_t(p)-H_s(p)\\geq\\kappa_g(s-t)\\mathrm{Id},\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\kappa_g&gt;0\\) depends only on the manifold. On every smooth minimizing branch,\n   \\[\n   A_x(w)w=w.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">3. **Transverse slack — original Lemma 2.3.**  \n   Let \\(p_i\\in G_x\\), \\(m_i&gt;0\\), \\(\\sum_i m_i=1\\), and put\n   \\[\n   p=\\sum_i m_ip_i,\\qquad E=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_i-p\\}.\n   \\]\n   If \\(t\\operatorname{conv}\\{p_i\\}\\subset I(x)\\) and \\(0&lt;t&lt;s&lt;1\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\left.\n   \\left[\n   \\frac{A_x(tp)}t-\\sum_i m_i\\frac{A_x(sp_i)}s\n   \\right]\\right|_{E^\\perp}\n   \\geq\\kappa_g(s-t)\\mathrm{Id}.\n   \\]\n   The conclusion also holds at a limiting time \\(t\\) if the radially contracted hulls lie in \\(I(x)\\) and the displayed Hessians have nonconjugate branch limits. No minimizing assumption about \\(sp\\) is needed in this limiting assertion.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. **Short time for a semiconvex datum — original Lemma 2.4.**  \n   If \\(f\\) is Lipschitz and semiconvex, then for sufficiently small \\(t&gt;0\\), depending only on these bounds and the manifold,\n   \\[\n   F_t:\\Gamma_f\\to M,\\qquad\n   \\Gamma_f=\\{(x,p):p\\in\\partial f(x)\\},\\qquad\n   F_t(x,p)=\\exp_x(tp),\n   \\]\n   is a homeomorphism with locally Lipschitz inverse into \\(TM\\). The minimizing pole \\(x_t(z)\\) of \\(Q_tf\\) is unique,\n   \\[\n   Q_tf\\in C^{1,1},\\qquad\n   \\operatorname{grad}Q_tf(z)=-t^{-1}\\log_zx_t(z),\n   \\]\n   and \\(\\Gamma_f\\) is an \\(n\\)-dimensional Lipschitz manifold.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. **Global supporting property — original Theorem 2.5.**  \n   For every potential \\(u\\), every \\(x\\in M\\), and every \\(p\\in\\partial u(x)\\), one has \\(p\\in G_x\\) and\n   \\[\n   u(x')\\geq u(x)+c(x,\\exp_xp)-c(x',\\exp_xp)\n   \\qquad(x'\\in M).\n   \\]\n   For every \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\), \\(F_t:\\Gamma_u\\to M\\) is a homeomorphism, \\(tp\\in I(x)\\), and its poles are the unique global minimizers of \\(Q_tu\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">6. **Convex injectivity domains — original Corollary 2.6.**  \n   Both \\(G_x\\) and \\(I(x)\\) are convex.</p><p class=\"li num\">7. **Convex lifted gap sections — original Corollary 2.7.**  \n   For a dual pair \\((u,v)\\), the set\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_x(r)=\n   \\left\\{p\\in G_x:\n   u(x)+\\frac12|p|^2+v(\\exp_xp)\\leq r\\right\\}\n   \\]\n   is compact and convex, including ordinary and conjugate cut endpoints. If it is nonempty and \\(r\\geq0\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{diam}\\mathcal S_x(r)^2\n   \\leq\n   8\\left(r+\n   \\operatorname{osc}_{p\\in\\mathcal S_x(r)}v(\\exp_xp)\\right).\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">8. **Intermediate regularity and pole control — original Proposition 2.8.**  \n   For a dual pair and \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\),\n   \\[\n   Q_tu=-Q_{1-t}v\\in C^{1,1}(M),\n   \\qquad\n   -\\frac{C_g}{1-t}g\n   \\leq\\operatorname{Hess}Q_tu\n   \\leq\\frac{C_g}{t}g\n   \\quad\\text{almost everywhere}.\n   \\]\n   If \\((x_t(z),p_t(z))=F_t^{-1}(z)\\) and \\(\\Phi_s\\) is geodesic flow, then\n   \\[\n   (x_t(z),p_t(z))\n   =\\Phi_{-t}(z,\\operatorname{grad}Q_tu(z)).\n   \\]\n   Thus the pole and momentum maps are Lipschitz, with constants depending only on \\((M,g),t\\). There are also \\(r_t,b_t&gt;0\\), with the same dependence, such that\n   \\[\n   u(x')+c_t(x',z)\n   \\geq u(x)+c_t(x,z)+b_td(x,x')^2\n   \\]\n   whenever \\(z=\\exp_x(tp)\\), \\(p\\in\\partial u(x)\\), and \\(d(x,x')&lt;r_t\\).\n   These constants are uniform for \\(t\\) in a compact subinterval of \\((0,1)\\).</p><p>No time-one injectivity is asserted for an arbitrary potential.</p>", "proof": "<p>These statements are the density-free input from the companion paper [14], not results reproved here.</p><p>For \\(p\\in G_x\\), \\(y_s=\\exp_x(sp)\\), and \\(0&lt;s&lt;1\\), divided action gives\n\\[\nc(z,\\exp_xp)\n\\leq c_s(z,y_s)+\\frac{1-s}{2}|p|^2,\n\\qquad\nc_s(x,y_s)=\\frac{s}{2}|p|^2.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nThe right side is a smooth upper support near \\(x\\). Splitting at time \\(1/2\\) gives a common supporting neighborhood and Hessian bound.</p><p>The uniform-support and active-log assertions are [14, Lemma 4.1]. The radial and transverse assertions are [14, Lemmas 4.2–4.3]; the transverse assertion applies to every positive finite representation. Short-time regularization is [14, Lemma 4.4]. Global support, convex injectivity domains, and convex lifted sections are [14, Theorem 4.8 and Corollaries 4.9–4.10]. Intermediate regularity and pole control are [14, Proposition 4.11], whose proof uses two-sided supports from the split contact geodesic and smooth backward geodesic flow, rather than inversion of the full endpoint exponential map.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Radial Jacobian comparison and isotropic pullback gain — original Corollary 3.3 and Lemma 3.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Radial Jacobian comparison and isotropic pullback gain — original Corollary 3.3 and Lemma 3.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((M,g)\\) be the fixed smooth compact Riemannian manifold. The assertions of this proposition use no MTW assumption.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(G_x,A_x,\\sigma_x\\) as in Proposition 1, with specified smooth branches at nonconjugate minimizing cut vectors.</p><p class=\"li\">Adjoints and singular values use the Riemannian inner products.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are positive constants depending only on \\((M,g)\\) with the following properties.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(v\\in G_x\\) and \\(1/2\\leq\\theta\\leq1\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_x(\\theta v)\\geq c\\,\\sigma_x(v).\n   \\tag{3.16}\n   \\]\n   If \\(y=\\exp_xp\\), \\(p\\in G_x\\), and \\(\\bar p=-P_{x\\to y}p\\), then\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_y(\\bar p/l)\\leq C\\sigma_x(p)\n   \\quad\\text{if }l\\in[1/2,1),\\ \\bar p/l\\in G_y,\n   \\tag{3.17}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_y(\\bar p/l_+)\\geq c\\sigma_x(p)\n   \\quad\\text{if }l_+\\in[1,2].\n   \\tag{3.18}\n   \\]\n   The longer reversed vector in (3.17) may have a conjugate endpoint.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Let \\(r\\in T_yM\\) and \\(1&lt;T_*\\leq2\\) satisfy \\(T_*r\\in G_y\\). Put\n   \\[\n   \\gamma(s)=\\exp_y(sr),\\quad\n   x=\\gamma(1),\\quad\n   p=-P_{y\\to x}r,\\quad\n   L=(d\\exp_x)_p,\\quad\n   \\tau=1+\\frac{T_*-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   All costs in\n   \\[\n   L^*\\left[A_y(r)-\\frac1\\tau A_y(\\tau r)\\right]L\n   \\succeq c_p(T_*-1)\\mathrm{Id}\n   \\tag{3.19}\n   \\]\n   are smooth, and this inequality holds. If the terminal ray at time \\(T_*\\) is nonconjugate, the same inequality holds with \\(\\tau\\) replaced by \\(T_*\\) and its chosen branch. In particular,\n   \\[\n   L^*[A_y(r)-lA_y(r/l)]L\n   \\succeq c_p(1-l)\\mathrm{Id},\n   \\qquad 1/2\\leq l&lt;1,\n   \\tag{3.20}\n   \\]\n   when \\(T_*=1/l\\) and that terminal branch is nonconjugate.</p><p>Without a uniformity assertion, the source Hessian of divided action decreases strictly in positive definite order between any two nonconjugate times on a minimizing ray.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(\\theta&lt;1\\), multiply the lower bounds in Lemma 2.2 with \\(\\delta=1-\\theta\\), and discard the nonnegative \\(\\delta\\)-terms. This proves (3.16); \\(\\theta=1\\) is immediate. Apply (3.16) to the longer vector \\(\\bar p/l\\), with shortening factor \\(l\\), and use reversal from Lemma 2.1 to obtain (3.17). Apply it to \\(\\bar p\\), with shortening factor \\(1/l_+\\), to obtain (3.18).</p><p>For the pullback gain, every time below \\(T_*\\) is strictly before cut. Thus \\(r,\\tau r\\in I(y)\\), and the reversed vector \\(p\\in I(x)\\). Let \\(J(s)\\) be the Jacobi matrix based at \\(y\\). Reversal gives\n\\[\nL=J(1)^*.\n\\tag{3.21}\n\\]\nLemma 2.2 applied to \\(T_*r\\), with shortening factor \\(1/T_*\\), gives\n\\[\ns_1(J(1))\\geq c(T_*-1),\n\\qquad\n\\|J(1)^{-1}\\|\\leq\\frac{C}{T_*-1}.\n\\tag{3.22}\n\\]\nSmooth Jacobi evolution bounds \\(\\|J'(s)\\|\\) uniformly for \\(0\\leq s\\leq T_*\\). Therefore, for \\(1\\leq s\\leq\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\|J(s)J(1)^{-1}\\|\n\\leq1+\\|J(s)-J(1)\\|\\|J(1)^{-1}\\|\n\\leq1+C\\frac{s-1}{T_*-1}\\leq C.\n\\tag{3.23}\n\\]\nWriting \\(S(s)=A_y(sr)/s\\), Lemma 2.1 gives\n\\[\nL^*[-S'(s)]L\n=J(1)J(s)^{-1}J(s)^{-*}J(1)^*.\n\\tag{3.24}\n\\]\nThis is \\(B_sB_s^*\\), where \\(B_s=J(1)J(s)^{-1}\\). Its smallest eigenvalue is\n\\(\\|J(s)J(1)^{-1}\\|^{-2}\\), uniformly positive by (3.23). Integration over \\([1,\\tau]\\), of length \\((T_*-1)/2\\), proves (3.19).</p><p>If the terminal branch is nonconjugate, add the positive definite integral over \\([\\tau,T_*]\\). Since \\(T_*-1=(1-l)/l\\geq1-l\\), this proves (3.20). Integration of\n\\[\n-S'(s)=J(s)^{-1}J(s)^{-*}\\succ0\n\\]\nalso proves strict decrease between nonconjugate times. At a conjugate terminal endpoint, only the half-extension formula is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Jacobi matrices and reversal — original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Jacobi matrices and reversal — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(\\gamma(s)=\\exp_x(sv)\\), identify tangent spaces by parallel transport, and put\n\\[\nR(s)w=R(w,\\dot\\gamma(s))\\dot\\gamma(s).\n\\]\nLet \\(C,J\\) solve\n\\[\nU''+RU=0,\\quad\nC(0)=\\mathrm{Id},\\ C'(0)=0,\\quad\nJ(0)=0,\\ J'(0)=\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(T&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n(d\\exp_x)_{Tv}=\\frac{J(T)}T.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nWhenever the geodesic to \\(\\gamma(T)\\) is nonconjugate, let \\(c_{\\gamma,T}\\) be its smooth local action, one half the squared length of the branch obtained by varying both endpoints. With the endpoint held fixed in the source Hessian,\n\\[\nS(T):=\nD^2_{xx}\\frac{c_{\\gamma,T}(x,\\gamma(T))}{T}\n=J(T)^{-1}C(T),\n\\qquad\nS'(T)=-J(T)^{-1}J(T)^{-*}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nThese identities apply whether or not the branch minimizes. Before cut the action is \\(c\\); at a nonconjugate minimizing cut vector it is the chosen smooth upper branch.</p><p>If \\(y=\\exp_xv\\) and \\(\\bar v=-P_{x\\to y}v\\), then\n\\[\n(d\\exp_y)_{\\bar v}=((d\\exp_x)_v)^*,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_y(\\bar v)=\\sigma_x(v).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nFinally, for \\(v\\in I(x)\\),\n\\[\nA_x(v)v=v.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Varying initial velocity gives (3.1). Since \\(R\\) is symmetric, the fundamental matrix\n\\[\n\\Phi(T)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}C(T)&amp;J(T)\\\\ C'(T)&amp;J'(T)\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nis symplectic. In particular,\n\\[\nJ^*J'=J'^*J,\\qquad\nC^*J'-C'^*J=\\mathrm{Id},\\qquad\nCJ^*=JC^*.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThese identities also follow by differentiating their left sides and evaluating at zero.</p><p>For initial-point variation \\(a\\) and terminal variation zero, the variational field is\n\\[\nC(s)a+J(s)b,\\qquad b=-J(T)^{-1}C(T)a.\n\\]\nThe initial gradient of divided action is negative initial velocity, giving \\(S(T)=J(T)^{-1}C(T)\\). The last identity in (3.5) makes this matrix symmetric. The first two give\n\\[\nJ^*(C'-J'J^{-1}C)\n=J^*C'-J'^*C=-\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dT}(J^{-1}C)\n=J^{-1}(C'-J'J^{-1}C)\n=-J^{-1}J^{-*}.\n\\]</p><p>Symplecticity also gives\n\\[\n\\Phi(T)^{-1}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nJ'(T)^*&amp;-J(T)^*\\\\\n-C'(T)^*&amp;C(T)^*\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nA reversed Jacobi field with initial position zero and initial derivative \\(a\\) has forward terminal data \\((0,-a)\\). Multiplication by this inverse shows that its value at the other endpoint is \\(J(T)^*a\\). Thus the reversed Jacobi matrix is \\(J(T)^*\\), and division by \\(T\\) proves (3.3), without requiring invertibility of \\(J(T)\\).</p><p>In the radial direction,\n\\[\nJ(s)v=sv,\\qquad C(s)v=v.\n\\]\nHence \\(S(T)v=v/T\\), and \\(T=1\\) gives (3.4). The case \\(v=0\\) is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform radial transversality — original Lemma 3.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform radial transversality — original Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>For \\(v\\in G_x\\), write\n\\[\ns_1(x,v)\\leq\\cdots\\leq s_n(x,v)\n\\]\nfor the singular values of \\((d\\exp_x)_v\\), including zero singular values at conjugacy. For a positive definite form \\(Q\\), let \\(\\lambda_j^\\downarrow(Q)\\) list eigenvalues in decreasing order.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(c_r&gt;0\\), \\(C_r&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on \\((M,g)\\), such that, for \\(0&lt;\\delta\\leq1/2\\),\n\\[\nc_r(s_j(x,v)+\\delta)\n\\leq s_j(x,(1-\\delta)v)\n\\leq C_r(s_j(x,v)+\\delta).\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nThere is a positive definite form \\(Q_{x,v,\\delta}\\) such that, with\n\\[\nN_{x,\\delta}(v)\n=-\\delta\\bigl(A_x((1-\\delta)v)-\\mathrm{Id}\\bigr),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\|N_{x,\\delta}(v)-Q_{x,v,\\delta}\\|\\leq C_r\\delta,\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nc_r\\frac{\\delta}{s_j(x,v)+\\delta}\n\\leq\\lambda_j^\\downarrow(Q_{x,v,\\delta})\n\\leq C_r\\frac{\\delta}{s_j(x,v)+\\delta}.\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n\\|N_{x,\\delta}(v)\\|\\leq C_r,\\qquad\nN_{x,\\delta}(v)\\succeq-C_r\\delta\\mathrm{Id},\\qquad\nN_{x,\\delta}(v)v=0.\n\\tag{3.9}\n\\]\nNo condition on the multiplicity of conjugacy is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>The minimizing-vector bundle\n\\[\n\\mathcal G=\\{(x,v):x\\in M,\\ v\\in G_x\\}\n\\]\nis compact. Consider \\(\\gamma(s)=\\exp_x(sv)\\) on \\([0,1]\\). Fix \\(h=1/4\\), and let \\(T\\in[1/2,1]\\). The prefix \\(hv\\) is before cut. For the suffix, reverse at \\(y_T=\\gamma(T)\\) and set\n\\[\nw_T=-(T-h)\\dot\\gamma(T),\\qquad\na_T=\\frac{T}{T-h}\\geq\\frac43.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\exp_{y_T}w_T=\\gamma(h),\\qquad\nd(y_T,\\exp_{y_T}(a_Tw_T))=a_T|w_T|,\n\\]\nso \\(w_T\\in I(y_T)\\). Reversal preserves uniqueness and, by Lemma 2.1, nonconjugacy. Compactness therefore bounds the two piece differentials and their inverses uniformly.</p><p>Let \\(C,J\\) be based at time zero, and let \\(C_h,J_h\\) have the same initial data at time \\(h\\). The matrices\n\\[\nJ(h),\\ J(h)^{-1},\\\nJ_h(T-h),\\ J_h(T-h)^{-1},\\\nC(h),\\ J'(h)\n\\tag{3.10}\n\\]\nhave uniformly bounded norms, including when \\(v=0\\).</p><p>For \\(z=\\gamma(h)\\), the joining-point Hessian of\n\\[\nz'\\longmapsto \\frac{c(x,z')}{h}+\\frac{c(z',y_T)}{T-h}\n\\]\nis\n\\[\nD(T)=J'(h)J(h)^{-1}\n     +J_h(T-h)^{-1}C_h(T-h).\n\\tag{3.11}\n\\]\nAt \\(T=1\\), the broken action has a minimum at \\(z\\), because\n\\[\n\\frac{d(x,z')^2}{2h}\n+\\frac{d(z',y_1)^2}{2(1-h)}\n\\geq\n\\frac{(d(x,z')+d(z',y_1))^2}{2}\n\\geq\\frac{d(x,y_1)^2}{2},\n\\]\nwith equality at \\(z\\). Thus \\(D(1)\\succeq0\\).</p><p>Only the second term of \\(D(T)\\) depends on \\(T\\). Lemma 2.1 gives\n\\[\nD(1-\\delta)=D(1)+\\delta G_\\delta,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nG_\\delta=\n\\frac1\\delta\\int_{1-\\delta}^1\nJ_h(s-h)^{-1}J_h(s-h)^{-*}\\,ds,\n\\qquad\nc\\mathrm{Id}\\preceq G_\\delta\\preceq C\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\tag{3.12}\n\\]\nThe crossing is positive on the whole space, including \\(\\ker D(1)\\).</p><p>Jacobi propagation gives the exact factorization\n\\[\nJ(T)\n=C_h(T-h)J(h)+J_h(T-h)J'(h)\n=J_h(T-h)D(T)J(h).\n\\tag{3.13}\n\\]\nThis remains valid when the full matrix at \\(T=1\\) is singular. By min–max,\n\\[\n\\lambda_j(D(1))+c\\delta\n\\leq\\lambda_j(D(1-\\delta))\n\\leq\\lambda_j(D(1))+C\\delta,\n\\]\nwhere these eigenvalues are increasing. Multiplication on either side by uniformly bounded invertible matrices changes each ordered singular value by only uniform factors. Apply this to (3.13), use \\((d\\exp_x)_{Tv}=J(T)/T\\), and obtain (3.6).</p><p>For the Hessian assertion, set\n\\[\nP=J(h)^{-1}C(h),\\qquad E=J(h)^{-*}.\n\\]\nThe source–join mixed Hessian is \\(-E^*\\). Eliminating the joining variation in the broken-action Hessian yields, for \\(T&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{A_x(Tv)}T=P-E^*D(T)^{-1}E.\n\\tag{3.14}\n\\]\nIndeed, the quadratic form\n\\[\na^*Pa-2a^*E^*b+b^*D(T)b\n\\]\nhas stationary point \\(b=D(T)^{-1}Ea\\) and stationary value\n\\(a^*(P-E^*D(T)^{-1}E)a\\).</p><p>For \\(T=1-\\delta\\),\n\\[\nN_{x,\\delta}(v)\n=Q_{x,v,\\delta}+\\delta(\\mathrm{Id}-TP),\n\\qquad\nQ_{x,v,\\delta}=T\\delta E^*D(T)^{-1}E.\n\\tag{3.15}\n\\]\nThe bounds on \\(P,E,E^{-1}\\), the preceding min–max inequalities, and inversion of \\(D(T)\\) prove (3.7)–(3.8), hence the norm and lower bounds in (3.9). The radial identity in Lemma 2.1 gives \\(N_{x,\\delta}(v)v=0\\).</p><p>For \\(v\\neq0\\), the radial eigenvalue of \\(N_{x,\\delta}(v)\\) is exactly zero, whereas the radial singular value of \\((d\\exp_x)_v\\) is one. The background term \\(\\delta(\\mathrm{Id}-TP)\\) in (3.15) is retained.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Jacobian comparison for barycentric configurations — original Corollary 3.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Jacobian comparison for barycentric configurations — original Corollary 3.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed weak-MTW manifold and the notation of Propositions 1–2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\mu\\in(0,1)\\) and \\(\\kappa,L_0&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  p=\\sum_{j=1}^N m_jp_j,\\qquad\n  p_j\\in G_x,\\quad m_j&gt;0,\\quad\\sum_jm_j=1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For one index \\(i\\), suppose there are \\(D&gt;0\\), a unit vector \\(e\\), and \\(t_i&gt;0\\) such that\n  \\[\n  p_i=p+t_ie,\\quad\n  h=p\\cdot e&gt;0,\\quad\n  h^2\\geq\\kappa D,\\quad\n  |p_i|^2-|p|^2\\leq L_0D,\\quad\n  m_i\\geq\\mu.\n  \\tag{3.37}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c&gt;0\\), depending only on \\((M,g),\\mu,\\kappa,L_0\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sigma_x(p)\\geq c\\,\\sigma_x(p_i).\n\\tag{3.38}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(q=t_ie\\). The norm increment gives\n\\[\n2ht_i+t_i^2\\leq L_0D,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{|q|^2}{p\\cdot q}\n=\\frac{t_i}{h}\n\\leq\\frac{L_0D}{2h^2}\n\\leq\\frac{L_0}{2\\kappa}.\n\\]\nSince \\(q\\neq0\\), \\(m_i&lt;1\\). By convexity of \\(G_x\\), the remaining barycenter belongs to \\(G_x\\):\n\\[\n\\frac1{1-m_i}\\sum_{j\\neq i}m_jp_j\n=p-\\frac{m_i}{1-m_i}q.\n\\]\nThe coefficient \\(m_i/(1-m_i)\\) is at least \\(\\mu\\). Hence \\(p+sq\\in G_x\\) for every \\(s\\in[-\\mu,1]\\). Apply Lemma 3.1 with\n\\[\na_0=\\mu,\\qquad B=L_0/(2\\kappa).\n\\]\nNo upper bound on the opposite-extension coefficient is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform comparison along an extended affine segment — original Lemma 3.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform comparison along an extended affine segment — original Lemma 3.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix \\(a_0&gt;0\\) and \\(B&lt;\\infty\\). Let \\(p,q\\in T_xM\\), \\(q\\neq0\\), satisfy\n\\[\np+tq\\in G_x\\quad(-a_0\\leq t\\leq1),\n\\qquad\np\\cdot q&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\frac{|q|^2}{p\\cdot q}\\leq B.\n\\tag{3.25}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c_s&gt;0\\), depending only on \\((M,g),a_0,B\\), such that\n\\[\ns_j(x,p)\\geq c_s s_j(x,p+q),\n\\qquad 1\\leq j\\leq n,\n\\tag{3.26}\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\sigma_x(p)\\geq c_s^n\\sigma_x(p+q).\n\\tag{3.27}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If an admissible configuration exists, then \\(B&gt;0\\). Set\n\\[\na=\\min\\{a_0,(2B)^{-1}\\}.\n\\]\nFirst suppose the segment lies in \\(I(x)\\). Write\n\\[\np(t)=p+tq,\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac{|q|^2}{p\\cdot q},\\qquad\nL(t)=\\mathrm{Id}+t\\frac{qq^*}{p\\cdot q}.\n\\]\nIts eigenvalues are \\(1\\) on \\(q^\\perp\\) and \\(1+t\\beta\\) on \\(\\mathbb Rq\\), so\n\\[\n\\|L(t)\\|\\leq1+B,\\qquad\n\\|L(t)^{-1}\\|\\leq2\n\\quad(-a\\leq t\\leq1).\n\\tag{3.28}\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\delta\\leq1/2\\), put\n\\[\nN_\\delta(t)\n=-\\delta\\bigl(A_x((1-\\delta)p(t))-\\mathrm{Id}\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nF_\\delta(t)=L(t)^*N_\\delta(t)L(t).\n\\tag{3.29}\n\\]\nLemma 2.2 gives uniform bounds and \\(F_\\delta(t)\\succeq-C\\delta\\mathrm{Id}\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(z\\in T_xM\\), the vector\n\\[\n\\xi=z-\\frac{z\\cdot q}{p\\cdot q}p\n\\]\nis perpendicular to \\(q\\), and\n\\[\n\\xi-L(t)z\n=-\\frac{z\\cdot q}{p\\cdot q}p(t).\n\\]\nThe last vector is in the radial kernel of \\(N_\\delta(t)\\). Thus\n\\[\nz^*F_\\delta(t)z=\\xi^*N_\\delta(t)\\xi.\n\\tag{3.30}\n\\]\nWeak MTW makes \\(t\\mapsto A_x((1-\\delta)p(t))(\\xi,\\xi)\\) concave, so \\(F_\\delta\\) is convex in Loewner order.</p><p>Even if \\(p\\cdot q\\) is small, (3.28) and (3.30) bound this scalar convex function by \\(C|z|^2\\), independently of the size of \\(\\xi\\). Secant-slope bounds on each compact \\(K\\subset(-a,1)\\), followed by polarization, give uniform interior Lipschitz bounds for every matrix entry.</p><p>Suppose (3.26) fails. After passage to a subsequence there are configurations indexed by \\(k\\), and one fixed \\(j\\), with\n\\[\n\\frac{s_j(x_k,p_k)}{s_j(x_k,p_k+q_k)}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{3.31}\n\\]\nUse orthonormal identifications with \\(\\mathbb R^n\\), and write\n\\[\ns_{i,k}(t)=s_i(x_k,p_k+tq_k),\\qquad\nR_k=\\log\\frac{s_{j,k}(1)}{s_{j,k}(0)}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nChoose a countable dense set \\(\\{t_1,t_2,\\ldots\\}\\) of \\((-a,1)\\), enlarged to include \\(0,1\\). Choose \\(\\log\\delta_k\\) in the middle half of\n\\[\n[\\log s_{j,k}(0),\\log s_{j,k}(1)]\n\\]\nafter excluding intervals of radius \\(R_k^{1/3}\\) centered at\n\\[\n\\log s_{i,k}(t_m),\n\\qquad\n1\\leq i\\leq n,\\quad\n1\\leq m\\leq\\lfloor R_k^{1/3}\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe excluded length is at most \\(2nR_k^{2/3}\\), while the available interval has length \\(R_k/2\\). Therefore\n\\[\ns_{j,k}(0)/\\delta_k\\to0,\\qquad\ns_{j,k}(1)/\\delta_k\\to\\infty,\n\\tag{3.32}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\left|\\log\\frac{s_{i,k}(t_m)}{\\delta_k}\\right|\n\\longrightarrow\\infty\n\\quad\\text{for every fixed }i,m.\n\\tag{3.33}\n\\]\nUniform upper bounds on singular values imply \\(\\delta_k\\to0\\).</p><p>Form \\(F_k\\) from (3.29) with scale \\(\\delta_k\\). Uniform boundedness and interior Lipschitz bounds yield a locally uniform limit \\(F(t)\\) on \\((-a,1)\\). Extract also \\(E=\\lim_kF_k(1)\\). Both are positive semidefinite, and \\(F\\) is bounded and matrix convex.</p><p>By Lemma 2.2,\n\\[\nF_k(t)=L_k(t)^*Q_{x_k,p_k+tq_k,\\delta_k}L_k(t)+E_k(t),\n\\qquad\n\\|E_k(t)\\|\\leq C\\delta_k.\n\\]\nThe \\(i\\)-th decreasing eigenvalue of the positive term is between uniform multiples of\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta_k}{s_{i,k}(t)+\\delta_k}.\n\\tag{3.34}\n\\]\nAt a fixed dense point \\(t_m\\), put \\(\\eta_k=e^{-R_k^{1/3}}\\). The scale construction says, eventually, that \\(s_{i,k}(t_m)/\\delta_k\\) is at most \\(\\eta_k\\) or at least \\(\\eta_k^{-1}\\). Weyl's inequality gives either\n\\[\n\\lambda_i^\\downarrow(F_k(t_m))\n\\geq\\frac{c_0}{1+\\eta_k}-C\\delta_k,\n\\]\nor\n\\[\n-C\\delta_k\n\\leq\\lambda_i^\\downarrow(F_k(t_m))\n\\leq C_0\\eta_k+C\\delta_k.\n\\]\nSince the matrices converge, each limiting eigenvalue is either zero or uniformly positive. Continuity and density therefore give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{spec}F(t)\n\\subset\\{0\\}\\cup[c_*,C_*]\n\\qquad(-a&lt;t&lt;1)\n\\tag{3.35}\n\\]\nfor fixed positive constants. Hence the rank is locally constant, and therefore constant on \\((-a,1)\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(z\\), bounded scalar convexity gives a finite limit of \\(z^*F(t)z\\) as \\(t\\uparrow1\\). Polarization gives \\(F(1-)\\), whose rank equals the interior rank by the spectral gap. For fixed \\(s&lt;t&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nF_k(t)\n\\preceq\\frac{1-t}{1-s}F_k(s)\n+\\frac{t-s}{1-s}F_k(1).\n\\]\nFirst let \\(k\\to\\infty\\), then \\(t\\uparrow1\\). Thus\n\\[\nF(1-)\\preceq E,\n\\tag{3.36}\n\\]\nso \\(\\operatorname{rank}E\\geq\\operatorname{rank}F(1-)\\).</p><p>By (3.32) and (3.34), \\(\\operatorname{rank}F(0)\\geq j\\), whereas \\(\\operatorname{rank}E\\leq j-1\\). This contradicts rank constancy and (3.36).</p><p>For a closed segment in \\(G_x\\), replace it by \\((1-\\varepsilon)(p+tq)\\). Radial shortening places it in \\(I(x)\\), without changing the extension interval or the ratio in (3.25). Let \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), use continuity of singular values, and multiply over \\(j\\). All constants depend only on the stated parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Reduction to a power bound for section oscillations — original Lemma 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Reduction to a power bound for section oscillations — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the fixed manifold and density bounds of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(f:M\\to\\mathbb R\\), use\n  \\[\n  f^c(x)=\\max_y\\{-c(x,y)-f(y)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C\\) be the class of cost-dual pairs \\((u,v)\\) for all admissible density pairs, with any fixed additive normalization.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  S_r(x;u,v)=\\{y:u(x)+c(x,y)+v(y)\\leq r\\},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  F(r)=\\sup_{(u,v)\\in\\mathcal C,\\ x\\in M}\n       \\operatorname{osc}_{S_r(x;u,v)}v.\n  \\]\n  These sections are nonempty compact sets, and \\(F\\) is bounded and nondecreasing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\nF(r)\\leq C_0r^\\beta\\qquad(0&lt;r&lt;r_0)\n\\]\nfor some \\(\\beta&gt;0\\), \\(C_0&lt;\\infty\\), \\(r_0&gt;0\\), then every optimal map in the fixed density class has a homeomorphic representative with a common Hölder estimate in both directions. Any exponent no larger than\n\\[\n\\frac12\\min\\{\\beta,1\\}\n\\]\nmay be used.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\nh_x(p)=u(x)+\\frac12|p|^2+v(\\exp_xp),\\qquad p\\in G_x.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, the lifted section \\(\\{h_x\\leq r\\}\\) is convex. For \\(p,q\\) in it and \\(m=(p+q)/2\\),\n\\[\n\\frac18|p-q|^2\n=v(\\exp_xm)-\\frac{v(\\exp_xp)+v(\\exp_xq)}2\n+\\frac{h_x(p)+h_x(q)}2-h_x(m)\n\\leq F(r)+r.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThe exponential map has a common Lipschitz bound on the compact minimizing-vector bundle. Thus the target diameter of a section is at most \\(C\\sqrt{F(r)+r}\\).</p><p>If \\((x,y)\\) and \\((x',y')\\) are contacts of the same dual pair, common Lipschitz bounds for \\(u,c\\) give\n\\[\n0\\leq u(x)+c(x,y')+v(y')\\leq Cd(x,x').\n\\]\nBoth \\(y,y'\\) belong to the resulting section at \\(x\\). For small \\(d(x,x')\\), (4.1) gives\n\\[\nd(y,y')\\leq C d(x,x')^{\\frac12\\min\\{\\beta,1\\}}.\n\\]\nLetting the height decrease to zero shows that each pole has exactly one contact endpoint, depending continuously on the pole.</p><p>The class contains the reversed pair \\((v,u)\\), after normalization. The same argument gives a unique continuous reverse contact. There is a contact above every point on both sides, so the two maps are inverse. They agree almost everywhere with the optimal maps by optimality and uniqueness. Compactness absorbs larger distances into the same uniform constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Separated scales and regularized interior maxima — original Lemma 4.6, with the scale and template constructions of Section 4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Separated scales and regularized interior maxima — original Lemma 4.6, with the scale and template constructions of Section 4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(\\mathcal C,S_r,F\\) from Proposition 4, and suppose \\(F\\) has no power upper bound near zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  \\varepsilon_0=2^{-10},\\qquad c_0=2^{-10},\\qquad c_1=c_0/4.\n  \\tag{4.6}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the center template \\(B_c\\) described in Lemma 5.2 below. Fix a cap \\(M_0\\geq2\\), then \\(K\\geq1\\), and then the outer template \\(B_o\\) and its parameter \\(\\eta\\) from that lemma. These choices precede passage to small scales.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a sequence of admissible pairs \\((u,v)\\), poles \\(x_0\\), heights \\(r&lt;R\\to0\\), and numbers \\(a,D\\), \\(D&gt;0\\), satisfying\n\\[\n\\min_{S_r(x_0;u,v)}v=a,\\qquad\n\\max_{S_r(x_0;u,v)}v=a+D,\n\\]\n\\[\nr/R\\to0,\\qquad\nF(R)\\leq(1+\\delta_j)D,\\quad\\delta_j\\to0,\\qquad\n|\\log D|/|\\log R|\\to0.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nb_-=r(R/r)^{1/3},\\qquad\nb_+=r(R/r)^{2/3},\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nand, for \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\),\n\\[\ns_y=\\frac{v(y)-a}{D},\\qquad\nw_{o,b}(y)=v(y)+bB_o(s_y),\\qquad\nw_{c,b}(y)=v(y)+bB_c(s_y),\n\\]\n\\[\ng_b=w_{o,b}^c,\\qquad\n\\widehat w_b=g_b^c=w_{o,b}^{cc},\\qquad\nE_b(y)=w_{c,b}(y)-\\widehat w_b(y),\n\\qquad\nE(b)=\\max_yE_b(y),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\pi(b)=\\frac{c_0}{4}b+\\frac{2b^2}{b_+}.\n\\tag{4.23}\n\\]</p><p>For every sufficiently small selected scale and every \\(t&lt;1\\) sufficiently close to one,\n\\[\n\\Phi_t(b,z)\n=Q_tg_b(z)+Q_{1-t}w_{c,b}(z)-\\pi(b)\n\\]\nhas a positive global maximum on \\([b_-,b_+]\\times M\\), with \\(b\\) interior. Both spatial terms are \\(C^{1,1}\\), and both minimizing points are unique.</p><p>If \\(x,y\\) are those minimizing points at a maximum, then there is \\(p\\in\\partial g_b(x)\\cap G_x\\) such that\n\\[\nz=\\exp_x(tp),\\qquad y=\\exp_xp,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_tg_b(z)+Q_{1-t}w_{c,b}(z)=E_b(y)&gt;0.\n\\tag{4.24}\n\\]\nMoreover, \\((x,y)\\) is before cut.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 supplies the selected sequence; no assumption that \\(D\\to0\\) is required. Lemmas 5.2–5.4 apply after the templates are fixed and scales are small.</p><p>At \\(b=b_+\\), (4.20) makes \\(E(b)-\\pi(b)&lt;0\\). At \\(b=b_-\\), it is at most \\(b_-\\). For\n\\[\nb_*=\\frac{c_0b_+}{8},\n\\]\nwhich lies in the interval at small scales,\n\\[\nE(b_*)-\\pi(b_*)\\geq c_0^2b_+/16.\n\\]\nSince \\(b_-/b_+\\to0\\), \\(E_b-\\pi\\) has a positive interior maximum separated from both amplitude boundaries.</p><p>For fixed scales and \\(b\\) in its compact interval,\n\\[\nQ_tg_b\\longrightarrow Q_1g_b=-\\widehat w_b\n\\]\nuniformly as \\(t\\uparrow1\\), directly from the uniform bound for \\(|c/t-c|\\). The functions \\(w_{c,b}\\) have a common fixed-scale Lipschitz constant \\(L_*\\), and\n\\[\nw_{c,b}-\\frac12L_*^2(1-t)\n\\leq Q_{1-t}w_{c,b}\\leq w_{c,b}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\Phi_t\\to E_b-\\pi\\) uniformly, preserving the positive interior maximum.</p><p>Proposition 1 gives \\(C^{1,1}\\) regularity and the unique pole for \\(Q_tg_b\\). The increasing scalar map\n\\[\nq\\longmapsto q+bB_c((q-a)/D)\n\\]\nhas bounded first two derivatives at fixed scales. Its composition with the Lipschitz semiconvex function \\(v\\) is semiconvex. A minimizer of \\(Q_\\epsilon w_{c,b}(z)\\) lies within \\(2L_*\\epsilon\\) of \\(z\\). For small \\(\\epsilon\\), all minimizers lie in a convex normal ball where the cost Hessian divided by \\(\\epsilon\\) dominates the negative semiconvexity bound. Strict convexity gives uniqueness.</p><p>In fixed coordinates, if \\(K_*\\) is a semiconvexity constant and \\(\\mu/\\epsilon\\) a lower cost-Hessian bound, the subgradient inequalities at neighboring minimizers give\n\\[\n(\\mu/\\epsilon-K_*)|y-y'|^2\n\\leq(C/\\epsilon)|z-z'|\\,|y-y'|.\n\\]\nHence the minimizer depends locally Lipschitzly on \\(z\\), and the envelope-gradient formula gives \\(C^{1,1}\\) regularity, uniformly in \\(b\\).</p><p>At a maximum, the two spatial gradients sum to zero. The envelope formulas show that the geodesic from \\(x\\) to \\(z\\), continued for time \\(1-t\\), reaches \\(y\\). The prefix minimum gives \\(p\\in\\partial g_b(x)\\). Proposition 1 makes the full vector minimizing and its endpoint a support for \\(g_b\\). Energy splitting gives (4.24).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nl=1+\\frac bD B_c'(s_y)\\in(0,1).\n\\]\nThe final short minimum gives its backward velocity in\n\\(\\partial w_{c,b}(y)=l\\,\\partial v(y)\\); this scalar chain rule follows by Taylor expansion and inversion of the increasing scalar map. Dividing by \\(l\\) yields a minimizing vector by Proposition 1. The backward vector to \\(x\\) is its strict radial shortening, so \\((x,y)\\) is before cut.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Logarithmic plateaus — original Lemma 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Logarithmic plateaus — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(F\\) be the section-oscillation supremum of Proposition 4, and suppose it has no power upper bound near zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(r_j&lt;R_j\\to0\\), \\((u_j,v_j)\\in\\mathcal C\\), poles \\(x_j\\), and \\(a_j,D_j\\), \\(D_j&gt;0\\), such that\n\\[\n\\min_{S_{r_j}(x_j;u_j,v_j)}v_j=a_j,\\qquad\n\\max_{S_{r_j}(x_j;u_j,v_j)}v_j=a_j+D_j,\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{r_j}{R_j}\\to0,\\qquad\nF(R_j)\\leq(1+\\delta_j)D_j,\\quad\\delta_j\\to0,\\qquad\n\\frac{|\\log D_j|}{|\\log R_j|}\\to0.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(R_j/D_j^k\\to0\\) for every fixed \\(k&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(F\\) vanished at a positive scale, monotonicity would give a power bound below that scale. Thus \\(F(r)&gt;0\\). Fix\n\\[\nC_F\\geq\\max\\{1,\\sup_rF(r)\\},\\qquad\nf(s)=\\log\\frac{C_F}{F(e^{-s})}.\n\\]\nThen \\(f\\) is nonnegative and nondecreasing. Failure of every power bound implies\n\\(\\liminf_{s\\to\\infty}f(s)/s=0\\). Choose \\(N_j\\to\\infty\\) with \\(f(N_j)=o(N_j)\\), and divide \\([N_j/2,N_j]\\) into\n\\[\nm_j=\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{N_j\\max\\{1,f(N_j)\\}}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\nequal intervals. Their lengths tend to infinity. Some interval \\([A_j,B_j]\\) has \\(f\\)-increment at most \\(f(N_j)/m_j=o(1)\\). Put\n\\[\nR_j=e^{-A_j},\\qquad r_j=e^{-B_j}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\log(R_j/r_j)\\to\\infty,\\qquad\n\\frac{F(R_j)}{F(r_j)}\n=e^{f(B_j)-f(A_j)}\\to1.\n\\]\nChoose an actual section with oscillation\n\\(D_j\\geq(1-1/j)F(r_j)\\). Its extrema are attained. Since\n\\(A_j\\geq N_j/2\\) and \\(f(B_j)\\leq f(N_j)=o(N_j)\\),\n\\[\n|\\log D_j|=o(A_j).\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\n\\log(R_j/D_j^k)=-A_j-k\\log D_j=-A_j+o(A_j)\\to-\\infty.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Smooth templates — original Lemma 4.3.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Smooth templates — original Lemma 4.3.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(\\varepsilon_0\\) from Proposition 5. Let \\(M_0\\geq2\\) and \\(K\\geq1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed smooth \\(B_c:\\mathbb R\\to(-1,1)\\), with bounded derivatives, such that\n\\[\nB_c'&lt;0,\\qquad\nB_c(s)\\geq1-\\varepsilon_0\\quad(s\\leq1/2),\\qquad\nB_c(s)&lt;0\\quad(s\\geq3/4).\n\\]\nThere are \\(0&lt;\\eta\\leq1/128\\) and a smooth bounded \\(B_o\\geq0\\), with bounded derivatives, such that\n\\[\nB_o(s)\\geq1\\quad(s\\leq1-\\eta),\\qquad\nB_o(1)=0,\\qquad\n1\\leq B_o(0)\\leq1+\\varepsilon_0,\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\n\\[\nB_o(s)\\geq B_o(0)+1\n\\quad(1/16\\leq s\\leq1-\\eta),\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nB_o'(s)&gt;0,\\qquad B_o''(s)\\leq-K\n\\]\nwhenever\n\\[\n-2\\eta\\leq s\\leq1-\\eta,\\qquad B_o(s)\\leq M_0.\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nOne can arrange \\(\\|B_o\\|_\\infty\\leq2M_0+8\\), independently of \\(K\\), and make \\(\\eta\\) arbitrarily smaller.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(\\gamma\\) so large that \\(2/(1+e^{\\gamma/8})\\leq\\varepsilon_0\\), and put\n\\[\nB_c(s)=1-\\frac{2}{1+e^{-\\gamma(s-5/8)}}.\n\\]\nIt has the asserted properties, and \\(|B_c'|\\) has a positive minimum on every fixed compact interval.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\beta(z)=\n\\begin{cases}e^{-1/z},&amp;z&gt;0,\\\\0,&amp;z\\leq0,\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\chi(z)=\\frac{\\beta(z)}{\\beta(z)+\\beta(1-z)}.\n\\]\nThis smooth step is zero on \\((-\\infty,0]\\) and one on \\([1,\\infty)\\). Set\n\\[\n\\kappa=K+1,\\quad A=M_0+3,\\quad\nL=128A\\kappa,\\quad h_0=\\frac1{128\\kappa},\n\\]\n\\[\n0&lt;\\eta\\leq\\min\\{1/128,\\varepsilon_0/(24L)\\}.\n\\]\nPut\n\\[\nw(s)=(L-\\kappa s)\\chi\\!\\left(\\frac{s+3\\eta}{\\eta}\\right)\n\\left[1-\\chi\\!\\left(\\frac{s-h_0}{h_0}\\right)\\right],\n\\]\n\\[\nJ(s)=1+\\frac{\\varepsilon_0}{2}+\\int_0^s w(q)\\,dq,\n\\qquad\nB_o(s)=J(s)\n\\left[1-\\chi\\!\\left(\\frac{s-(1-\\eta)}{\\eta}\\right)\\right].\n\\]\nThe nonnegative function \\(w\\) is supported in \\([-3\\eta,2h_0]\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_{-3\\eta}^0w\n\\leq3\\eta(L+3\\kappa\\eta)\n\\leq6\\eta L\\leq\\varepsilon_0/4,\n\\qquad\n\\int_0^{2h_0}w\\leq2Lh_0=2A.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n1+\\varepsilon_0/4\\leq J(s)\\leq1+\\varepsilon_0/2+2A.\n\\]\nOn \\([-2\\eta,h_0]\\),\n\\[\nJ'=L-\\kappa s&gt;0,\\qquad J''=-\\kappa,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nJ(h_0)-J(0)=A-\\frac1{32768\\kappa}&gt;M_0+1.\n\\]\nSince \\(J\\) is nondecreasing, \\(B_o(s)\\leq M_0\\) on \\([-2\\eta,1-\\eta]\\) forces \\(s&lt;h_0\\), where \\(B_o=J\\). This proves (4.9). The function \\(J\\) is constant beyond \\(2h_0\\leq1/64\\), giving (4.8). The final factor is one up to \\(1-\\eta\\) and decreases to zero only in \\((1-\\eta,1)\\). All remaining assertions follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**First switch with absolute level control — original Lemma 4.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**First switch with absolute level control — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the scales, potentials, and fixed templates of Proposition 5. Choose extreme endpoints \\(y_0,y_1\\) of the selected section, with\n\\[\nv(y_0)=a,\\qquad v(y_1)=a+D,\n\\]\nand minimizing logs \\(p_0,p_1\\in G_{x_0}\\). Set \\(P=|p_0|^2\\) and \\(H_o=\\|B_o\\|_\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently small selected scales, uniformly for \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\), there is\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{32D}\\leq\\tau_b\\leq\\frac{8b}{D}\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nsuch that, at \\(x_b=\\exp_{x_0}(\\tau_bp_0)\\), \\(g_b\\) has active endpoints \\(y_-,y_+\\) satisfying\n\\[\n-2\\eta\\leq s_{y_-}&lt;1/16,\\qquad\ns_{y_+}\\geq1-\\eta.\n\\]\nThe endpoints \\(y_0,y_1\\) and every active endpoint at this pole lie in a common original section \\(S_{Cb}(x_b;u,v)\\), with \\(C\\) depending only on the geometry and \\(H_o\\). Every active level belongs to \\([-\\delta_j,1+\\delta_j]\\). For every minimizing log \\(q_i\\) to an active endpoint,\n\\[\n|q_i|^2=P-2Ds_{y_i}+O_{H_o}(\\tau_b+b)\n       =P-2Ds_{y_i}+o(D).\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]\nThe constants in (4.14) are independent of \\(K\\) and of the height and derivatives of \\(B_o\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The scale selection gives, uniformly for \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\) and \\(0\\leq\\tau\\leq8b/D\\),\n\\[\n\\frac rb\\to0,\\quad\\frac bR\\to0,\\quad\n\\frac b{D^k}\\to0\\ (k&gt;0),\\quad\n\\frac\\tau D\\to0,\\quad\\frac{\\tau^2}{b}\\to0.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nAfter the templates are fixed, it also gives\n\\[\n\\delta_j=o(\\eta),\\qquad\n\\frac bD\\max\\{\\|B_o'\\|_\\infty,\\|B_c'\\|_\\infty\\}\\to0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac b{D^2}\n\\max\\{\\|B_o''\\|_\\infty,\\|B_c''\\|_\\infty\\}\\to0.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nBoth scalar modifications are therefore increasing.</p><p>Midpoint splitting gives a geometric constant and common radius such that, for any minimizing log \\(q\\) from \\(x\\) to \\(y\\),\n\\[\nc(\\exp_xz,y)\\leq c(x,y)-\\langle q,z\\rangle+C_g|z|^2.\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nAlong a smooth curve, the one-sided derivative of \\(-c(\\cdot,y)\\) is the maximum pairing with its minimizing logs. Wherever the restriction is differentiable,\n\\[\n\\frac d{d\\tau}[-c(x_\\tau,y)]\n\\leq d(x_\\tau,y)|\\dot x_\\tau|.\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nThe same upper derivative bound holds for an active mountain of a differentiable maximum: semiconvex one-sided derivatives must coincide with the derivative of that maximum.</p><p>Set\n\\[\nx_\\tau=\\exp_{x_0}(\\tau p_0),\\qquad\n0\\leq\\tau\\leq T=8b/D,\n\\]\nand let \\(\\Pi_\\tau\\) be parallel transport. Define\n\\[\nf_y(\\tau)=-c(x_\\tau,y)-v(y),\n\\]\n\\[\nf_0(\\tau)=-\\frac12(1-\\tau)^2P-a,\\qquad\nq_0(\\tau)=(1-\\tau)\\Pi_\\tau p_0.\n\\]\nSince the initial gaps at \\(y_0,y_1\\) lie in \\([0,r]\\),\n\\[\nP-|p_1|^2=2D+O(r),\\qquad\n|p_0-p_1|^2\\leq8(D+r),\\qquad\nP-\\langle p_0,p_1\\rangle\\leq6D.\n\\tag{4.16}\n\\]\nIn particular \\(P\\geq D\\) for small scales. Also\n\\[\nu-bH_o\\leq g_b\\leq u.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(1/16\\leq s_y\\leq1-\\eta\\), put\n\\[\nh_y(\\tau)\n=f_y(\\tau)-bB_o(s_y)-f_0(\\tau)+bB_o(0).\n\\]\nInitially \\(h_y(0)\\leq r-b\\). Wherever \\(h_y\\geq-b/2\\), every minimizing log \\(q_y\\) satisfies\n\\[\n|q_y|^2-|q_0|^2\n=-2Ds_y-2b(B_o(s_y)-B_o(0))-2h_y\n\\leq-D/8-b.\n\\]\nUsing (4.13) gives \\(h_y'\\leq-D/16\\) almost everywhere there. Thus the positive part of \\(h_y+b/2\\) is nonincreasing and starts at zero. No middle-level mountain is active.</p><p>Let \\(G_L,G_R\\) be the maxima over \\(s_y\\leq1/16\\) and \\(s_y\\geq1-\\eta\\). They are continuous, and\n\\[\ng_b(x_\\tau)=\\max\\{G_L(\\tau),G_R(\\tau)\\}.\n\\]\nInitially\n\\[\nG_L(0)\\leq u(x_0)-b,\\qquad\nG_R(0)\\geq f_1(0)\\geq u(x_0)-r.\n\\]\nWhile \\(G_R&gt;G_L\\), every active endpoint is right, and comparison with the left modified mountain gives\n\\[\n|q_y|^2\\leq|q_0|^2-2D(1-\\eta)+2bB_o(0)\n\\leq|q_0|^2-D.\n\\]\nRationalizing the square roots in (4.13),\n\\[\n\\frac d{d\\tau}\\{g_b(x_\\tau)-f_0(\\tau)\\}\n\\leq-D/2\n\\]\nalmost everywhere while \\(G_R&gt;G_L\\). This difference starts at most \\(r\\) and is always at least \\(-bB_o(0)\\geq-2b\\). It cannot satisfy this inequality throughout \\([0,T]\\). At the first equality time \\(\\tau_b\\leq T\\), both maxima are attained, and\n\\[\n-bB_o(0)\\leq g_b(x_\\tau)-f_0(\\tau)\\leq r\n\\qquad(0\\leq\\tau\\leq\\tau_b).\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]</p><p>By (4.11), \\(u(x_\\tau)-f_0(\\tau)\\leq bH_o+r\\). Equations (4.12) and (4.16) give\n\\[\nf_1(\\tau)\n\\geq f_0(0)-r+(P-6D)\\tau-C_g\\tau^2\n=:L(\\tau).\n\\tag{4.18}\n\\]\nSince \\(f_0(\\tau)=f_0(0)+P\\tau-P\\tau^2/2\\),\n\\[\nf_0-f_1\\leq r+6D\\tau+C\\tau^2=O(b).\n\\]\nThus \\(y_0,y_1\\) lie in an original section of height \\(Cb\\). Every active endpoint satisfies\n\\[\nu(x_\\tau)+c(x_\\tau,y)+v(y)\n=u(x_\\tau)-g_b(x_\\tau)-bB_o(s_y)\\leq bH_o.\n\\]\nThis common section has height below \\(R\\), contains levels \\(a,a+D\\), and has oscillation at most \\((1+\\delta_j)D\\). Hence every active level is in \\([-\\delta_j,1+\\delta_j]\\).</p><p>Keep a winning left endpoint \\(y_-\\) at the switch fixed, and put\n\\[\nk(\\tau)=f_{y_-}(\\tau)-bB_o(s_{y_-})-L(\\tau).\n\\]\nThen \\(k(0)\\leq2r-b&lt;-b/2\\), while \\(k(\\tau_b)\\geq0\\). Let \\(\\theta\\) be the last time before \\(\\tau_b\\) with \\(k=-b/2\\). On \\([\\theta,\\tau_b]\\), the level bound \\(s_{y_-}\\geq-2\\eta\\) gives\n\\[\n|q_{y_-}|^2-|q_0|^2\n\\leq4\\eta D+2r+12D\\tau+2C_g\\tau^2+b\n\\leq D.\n\\]\nRationalizing again,\n\\[\nf_{y_-}'\\leq(1-\\tau)P+2D\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Since \\(L'=P-6D-2C_g\\tau\\), one has \\(k'\\leq10D\\), and therefore\n\\[\nb/2\\leq k(\\tau_b)-k(\\theta)\n\\leq10D(\\tau_b-\\theta).\n\\]\nThis implies the stated lower bound in (4.14), without a template-height or derivative bound.</p><p>Finally, activity at the switch gives\n\\[\n|q_i|^2\n=(1-\\tau_b)^2P-2Ds_{y_i}-2bB_o(s_{y_i})\n-2\\{g_b(x_b)-f_0(\\tau_b)\\}.\n\\]\nEquations (4.17) and (4.5) prove (4.15).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Uniform positive excess — original Lemma 4.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform positive excess — original Lemma 4.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed templates, selected scales, and excess \\(E(b)\\) of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all sufficiently small selected scales, uniformly for \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\),\n\\[\nc_0b\\leq E(b)\\leq b,\n\\tag{4.20}\n\\]\nwith \\(c_0\\) independent of \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Order reversal of one cost transform and the double-transform inequality give\n\\[\nv\\leq\\widehat w_b\\leq w_{o,b},\n\\qquad\nE_b(y)\\leq bB_c(s_y)\\leq b.\n\\tag{4.19}\n\\]\nThis proves the upper bound.</p><p>At the switch in Lemma 5.3, the two active logs have squared norms on opposite sides of \\(P-D/2\\). Choose a point \\(p\\) on their segment with\n\\[\n|p|^2=P-D/2.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 1, \\(p\\) is minimizing and \\(y=\\exp_{x_b}p\\) is a contact endpoint for \\(g_b\\). Hence\n\\[\nE_b(y)=v(y)+bB_c(s_y)+c(x_b,y)+g_b(x_b).\n\\]\nMinorizing \\(g_b\\) by the left modified mountain,\n\\[\nE_b(y)\n\\geq D(s_y-1/4)+P\\tau_b-P\\tau_b^2/2\n+b\\{B_c(s_y)-B_o(0)\\}.\n\\tag{4.21}\n\\]\nIf \\(s_y&gt;1/2\\), this is at least \\(D/4-3b&gt;c_0b\\) for small scales.</p><p>If \\(s_y\\leq1/2\\), the original support inequality at \\(x_0\\) gives\n\\[\nv(y)-a\\geq c(x_0,y_0)-c(x_0,y)-r.\n\\]\nApply (4.12) at \\(x_b\\), with backward displacement\n\\(-\\tau_b\\Pi_{\\tau_b}p_0\\):\n\\[\nc(x_b,y)-c(x_0,y)\n\\geq-\\tau_b\\langle p,\\Pi_{\\tau_b}p_0\\rangle-C\\tau_b^2.\n\\]\nThe corresponding cost difference for \\(y_0\\) is\n\\(-P\\tau_b+P\\tau_b^2/2\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{E_b(y)}b\n\\geq B_c(s_y)-B_o(0)\n+\\bigl(P-\\langle p,\\Pi_{\\tau_b}p_0\\rangle\\bigr)\\frac{\\tau_b}{b}\n-\\frac rb-C\\frac{\\tau_b^2}{b}.\n\\tag{4.22}\n\\]\nThe norm choice gives\n\\[\nP-\\langle p,\\Pi_{\\tau_b}p_0\\rangle\n=\\frac{P-|p|^2+|p-\\Pi_{\\tau_b}p_0|^2}{2}\n\\geq D/4.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(\\tau_b\\geq b/(32D)\\), this contributes at least \\(1/128\\). The template difference is at least \\(-2\\varepsilon_0\\), and the last errors tend to zero by (4.5). The numerical choices give \\(E_b(y)\\geq c_0b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Universal parameter inequality and level control — original Lemmas 4.7–4.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Universal parameter inequality and level control — original Lemmas 4.7–4.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix sufficiently small scales and templates as in Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((b,z)\\) be an exact maximum supplied there, with minimizing pole \\(x\\), center endpoint \\(y=\\exp_xp\\), and \\(z=\\exp_x(tp)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  p=\\sum_{i=1}^Nm_ip_i,\\qquad\n  m_i&gt;0,\\quad\\sum_im_i=1,\n  \\]\n  be any finite representation by minimizing logs active for the displayed datum \\(g_b=w_{o,b}^c\\):\n  \\[\n  g_b(x)+c(x,\\exp_xp_i)+w_{o,b}(\\exp_xp_i)=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(y_i=\\exp_xp_i\\), \\(s_i=(v(y_i)-a)/D\\), \\(s=(v(y)-a)/D\\), and \\(H_o=\\|B_o\\|_\\infty\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every such representation,\n\\[\nB_c(s)-\\sum_im_iB_o(s_i)\\geq\\pi'(b)\\geq c_1.\n\\tag{4.25}\n\\]\nThe center and every active endpoint belong to\n\\(S_{(H_o+2)b}(x;u,v)\\), and\n\\[\nv(y_i)-v(y)\n=\\frac{|p|^2-|p_i|^2}{2}\n+b\\{B_c(s)-B_o(s_i)\\}-E_b(y).\n\\tag{4.30}\n\\]\nThe total weight on \\(s_i\\geq1-\\eta\\) is at least \\(c_1\\). Moreover,\n\\[\ns&lt;3/4,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{osc}\\{s,s_1,\\ldots,s_N\\}\\leq1+\\delta_j,\n\\qquad\n\\min\\{s,s_1,\\ldots,s_N\\}\\geq-2\\eta.\n\\tag{4.31}\n\\]\nThese conclusions hold before a prefix-time limit is taken.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(t,z\\), and put\n\\[\nA(b)=Q_tg_b(z),\\qquad C(b)=Q_{1-t}w_{c,b}(z).\n\\]\nDecrease \\(b\\) by \\(h&gt;0\\), and let \\(x_h\\) be the new prefix pole. Since\n\\(0\\leq g_{b-h}-g_b\\leq hH_o\\), compactness and uniqueness imply \\(x_h\\to x\\). Proposition 1 gives, for \\(\\Delta_h=\\exp_x^{-1}x_h\\),\n\\[\n|\\Delta_h|=O(\\sqrt h),\\qquad\n0\\leq A(b-h)-A(b)\\leq hH_o.\n\\tag{4.26}\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(q\\in(t,1)\\), and split the active endpoint costs at\n\\(z_i=\\exp_x(qp_i)\\). The upper support\n\\[\nc(x',y_i)\\leq c_q(x',z_i)+c_{1-q}(z_i,y_i)\n\\]\nis an equality at \\(x'=x\\). Its first term has gradient \\(-p_i\\) and Hessian \\(A_x(qp_i)/q\\). Combining with the smooth prefix cost gives\n\\[\nA(b-h)\\geq A(b)+hB_o(s_i)\n+\\langle p_i-p,\\Delta_h\\rangle\n+\\frac12Q_i(\\Delta_h,\\Delta_h)+o(|\\Delta_h|^2),\n\\tag{4.27}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nQ_i=\\frac1tA_x(tp)-\\frac1qA_x(qp_i).\n\\]\nAt fixed scales, time, and representation, (4.26)–(4.27) bound each linear pairing above by \\(O(h)\\). Their positive weighted sum is zero, so they are also bounded below by \\(-O(h)\\). With\n\\(E=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_i-p\\}\\),\n\\[\n|\\operatorname{proj}_E\\Delta_h|=O(h).\n\\tag{4.28}\n\\]\nTransverse MTW concavity and radial divided-action monotonicity give\n\\[\n\\sum_im_iQ_i\n=\\frac1tA_x(tp)-\\sum_i\\frac{m_i}{q}A_x(qp_i)\n\\geq0\\quad\\text{on }E^\\perp.\n\\tag{4.29}\n\\]\nAverage (4.27). Linear terms cancel. The transverse quadratic term is nonnegative; mixed terms are \\(O(h^{3/2})\\), the remaining terms \\(O(h^2)\\), and remainders \\(o(h)\\). Thus\n\\[\nA(b-h)-A(b)\\geq h\\sum_im_iB_o(s_i)+o(h).\n\\]\nUniqueness and convergence of the center minimizer give\n\\[\nC(b-h)-C(b)=-hB_c(s)+o(h).\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\) is an interior maximum of \\(A+C-\\pi\\), divide the resulting one-sided inequality by \\(h\\) and pass to zero. This proves (4.25) for the arbitrary representation.</p><p>For level control, an active endpoint has original gap\n\\[\nu-g_b-bB_o(s_i)\\in[0,bH_o].\n\\]\nThe center gap, by (4.24), is\n\\[\nu-g_b+E_b(y)-bB_c(s)\\leq(H_o+2)b.\n\\]\nThis proves the common-section assertion and its oscillation bound. Subtraction of the active equality from (4.24) gives (4.30). Since\n\\(0&lt;E_b(y)\\leq bB_c(s)\\), one has \\(s&lt;3/4\\).</p><p>If \\(m_R\\) is the total weight on \\(s_i\\geq1-\\eta\\), then\n\\[\n1-m_R\\leq\\sum_im_iB_o(s_i)\n\\leq B_c(s)-c_1\\leq1-c_1.\n\\]\nThus \\(m_R\\geq c_1\\). A right endpoint is present, and the common range bound puts every level above\n\\[\n(1-\\eta)-(1+\\delta_j)=-\\eta-\\delta_j\\geq-2\\eta.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Quantitative outward endpoint — original Lemma 4.9.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Quantitative outward endpoint — original Lemma 4.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in an \\(n\\)-dimensional Euclidean space.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  p=\\sum_{i=1}^Nm_ip_i,\\quad\n  N\\leq n+1,\\quad m_i&gt;0,\\quad\\sum_im_i=1,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  E=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_i-p\\}=\\mathbb Re,\\quad\n  |e|=1,\\quad q=p\\cdot e&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use the templates and \\(c_1,\\eta\\) of Proposition 5. Let \\(D&gt;0\\), and suppose\n  \\[\n  B_c(s)-\\sum_im_iB_o(s_i)\\geq c_1,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  s&lt;3/4,\\quad\n  \\operatorname{osc}\\{s,s_1,\\ldots,s_N\\}\\leq1+\\delta_j,\\quad\n  \\min\\{s,s_1,\\ldots,s_N\\}\\geq-2\\eta,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\left||p_i|^2-|p|^2+2D(s_i-s)\\right|\\leq\\zeta D,\n  \\qquad\n  0\\leq\\delta_j\\leq\\zeta\\leq1/16.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an outward index \\(i\\), with \\(p_i=p+d_ie\\), \\(d_i&gt;0\\), such that\n\\[\nm_i\\geq m_*:=\\frac{c_1}{12(n+1)},\\qquad\nm_id_i\\geq\\frac{c_1D}{8(n+1)q},\\qquad\nd_i\\leq\\frac{3D}{2q},\n\\tag{4.32}\n\\]\n\\[\n0&lt;|p_i|^2-|p|^2\\leq3D,\n\\tag{4.33}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n-2\\eta\\leq s_i&lt;1-\\eta,\\qquad\nB_o(s_i)\\leq1/m_*.\n\\tag{4.34}\n\\]\nThus the cap \\(M_0=2/m_*=24(n+1)/c_1\\) can be fixed before \\(K\\). These constants are independent of \\(K\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(p_i=p+d_ie\\). As in Proposition 6, total right mass is at least \\(c_1\\). A right point has \\(s_i-s&gt;1/4-\\eta\\), hence\n\\[\n|p_i|^2-|p|^2=2qd_i+d_i^2\\leq-D/4.\n\\]\nThus \\(d_i&lt;0\\) and \\(|d_i|\\geq D/(8q)\\).</p><p>For every outward point, the common level range gives\n\\[\n0&lt;2qd_i+d_i^2\n\\leq2D(1+\\delta_j)+\\zeta D\\leq3D,\n\\]\nso \\(d_i\\leq3D/(2q)\\). Since \\(\\sum_im_id_i=0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{d_i&gt;0}m_id_i\\geq\\frac{c_1D}{8q}.\n\\]\nChoose an outward index contributing at least \\(1/N\\) of this moment. Its upper displacement bound gives\n\\(m_i\\geq c_1/(12N)\\geq m_*\\), proving (4.32).</p><p>The parameter inequality and \\(B_o\\geq0\\) give \\(m_iB_o(s_i)\\leq1\\), proving the cap. The lower level bound is assumed. Positive norm increment gives\n\\[\ns_i\\leq s+\\zeta/2&lt;3/4+1/32&lt;1-\\eta.\n\\]\nThus the selected endpoint lies in the region of outer positive slope and negative curvature.</p><p>For convergent active representations, a selected limiting endpoint carries a definite positive moment and weight. Its strict outward displacement and strict separation from the right region persist for corresponding approximating vertices.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "contact measure, lower tests, and the almost-everywhere Jacobian — original Lemma 5.2 and equation (5.4)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u,v\\) be dual potentials for an admissible density pair from Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\mathscr C=\\{(x,y):u(x)+c(x,y)+v(y)=0\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A lower Taylor jet \\((\\zeta,B)\\) for \\(f\\) at a point, in local coordinates, means\n  \\[\n  f(h)\\geq f(0)+\\zeta\\cdot h+\\frac12B(h,h)+o(|h|^2).\n  \\]\n  Replacing \\(B\\) by \\(B-\\epsilon\\mathrm{Id}\\) gives a genuine local quadratic lower test.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If a \\(C^2\\) function \\(\\varphi\\) touches \\(v\\) from below at \\(y\\), with\n\\[\nr=\\operatorname{grad}\\varphi(y)\\in I(y),\\qquad\nW=\\operatorname{Hess}\\varphi(y)+A_y(r)&gt;0,\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\sigma_y(r)\\det W\\leq\\Lambda/\\lambda.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nThe same holds for a lower Taylor jet with these properties.</p><p>There is also a volume-null Borel set \\(N\\subset M\\) such that, at every \\(y\\notin N\\), \\(v\\) has an Alexandrov Hessian, its true log \\(r=\\operatorname{grad}v(y)\\) is before cut, and\n\\[\nW_v(y)=\\operatorname{Hess}v(y)+A_y(r)&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{\\Lambda}\n\\leq\\sigma_y(r)\\det W_v(y)\n\\leq\\frac{\\Lambda}{\\lambda}.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThe set \\(N\\) may include the exceptional sets in the contact-map differential of [2, Proposition 4.1(b)]. No density bound is asserted for a modified potential or its envelope.</p>", "proof": "<p>Decrease the test Hessian slightly, so that\n\\[\nv-\\varphi\\geq\\epsilon d(\\cdot,y)^2\n\\]\nin a small coordinate ball. The map\n\\[\nS(z)=\\exp_z\\operatorname{grad}\\varphi(z)\n\\]\nis a local diffeomorphism near \\(y\\). Differentiating\n\\[\nD_zc(z,S(z))+\\operatorname{grad}\\varphi(z)=0\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n|\\det DS(z)|\n=\\sigma_z(\\operatorname{grad}\\varphi(z))\n\\det\\{\\operatorname{Hess}\\varphi(z)\n+A_z(\\operatorname{grad}\\varphi(z))\\}.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nThe relative Hessian remains positive and the logs remain before cut on a smaller ball.</p><p>For \\(h&gt;0\\), let \\(\\Omega_h\\) be the component containing \\(y\\) of \\(\\{\\varphi+h&gt;v\\}\\) in that ball. It is relatively compact and shrinks to \\(y\\). For \\(z\\in\\Omega_h\\), set \\(x=S(z)\\), and define\n\\[\nm_x(w)=\\varphi(z)+c(z,x)-c(w,x).\n\\]\nThis mountain is tangent to \\(\\varphi\\) at \\(z\\). On a fixed smaller ball, \\(\\varphi-m_x\\) has positive Hessian, so \\(\\varphi\\geq m_x\\). On \\(\\partial\\Omega_h\\),\n\\(v-m_x\\geq h\\), while at \\(z\\) the difference is below \\(h\\). Its minimum is attained at an interior \\(z'\\). Thus\n\\(\\operatorname{grad}m_x(z')\\in\\partial v(z')\\). Proposition 1 turns this local contact into a global contact with endpoint \\(x\\). Hence\n\\[\nS(\\Omega_h)\\subset\\mathscr C(\\Omega_h).\n\\]\nThe area formula and Lemma 8.1 give\n\\[\n\\inf_{\\Omega_h}|\\det DS|\\,\\mathrm{vol}(\\Omega_h)\n\\leq\\mathrm{vol}(S(\\Omega_h))\n\\leq(\\Lambda/\\lambda)\\mathrm{vol}(\\Omega_h).\n\\]\nLet \\(h\\downarrow0\\), then restore the original Hessian. Taylor jets follow by the same decreasing-Hessian approximation.</p><p>The almost-everywhere statement is the consequence of [2, Proposition 4.1 and Theorem 4.2] recorded in the paper. Their Jacobian equation requires only absolute continuity with \\(L^1\\) densities. The positive lower density bound makes the full-source-measure set a full-volume set.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Contact-volume comparison — original Lemma 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Contact-volume comparison — original Lemma 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the original dual pair and contact relation \\(\\mathscr C\\) of Proposition 8. For a set on either side, let \\(\\mathscr C(A)\\) be its contact image, interpreting projections in completed volume measures.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every Borel set \\(A\\) on either side,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\lambda}{\\Lambda}\\mathrm{vol}(A)\n\\leq\\mathrm{vol}(\\mathscr C(A))\n\\leq\\frac{\\Lambda}{\\lambda}\\mathrm{vol}(A).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The contact relation is closed; projections of Borel sets are analytic and measurable in completed measures. The optimal coupling has full mass on \\(\\mathscr C\\).</p><p>At a differentiability point of either potential, all minimizing first-variation logs at a contact equal its gradient, so the contact endpoint is unique. Absolute continuity of both marginals implies that almost every contact pair consists of differentiability points on both sides. The corresponding maps are inverse.</p><p>Writing \\(\\mu=\\rho_0\\mathrm{vol}\\), \\(\\nu=\\rho_1\\mathrm{vol}\\), one obtains\n\\[\n\\nu(\\mathscr C(A))=\\mu(A)\n\\]\non the first side, and the reversed equality on the second. Additional contact image outside the almost-everywhere map image is, up to a null set, contained in the nondifferentiability set of the opposite potential. The density bounds give (5.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Exact pole matrix and endpoint lower jets — original Lemma 5.3 and equations (5.11)–(5.13).** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Exact pole matrix and endpoint lower jets — original Lemma 5.3 and equations (5.11)–(5.13).**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(w_o\\) be continuous,\n  \\[\n  g(x)=\\max_y\\{-c(x,y)-w_o(y)\\},\\qquad F=Q_tg,\\quad0&lt;t&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At \\(z_0\\), suppose \\(F\\) has a second-order expansion and its unique pole map \\(x(z)\\) is differentiable.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(x=x(z_0)\\), \\(z_0=\\exp_x(tp)\\), and choose\n  \\[\n  p=\\sum_{j=1}^Nm_jp_j,\\quad\n  m_j&gt;0,\\quad\\sum_jm_j=1,\\quad N\\leq n+1,\n  \\]\n  with \\(y_j=\\exp_xp_j\\) active for \\(w_o\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(E=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_j-p\\}\\). In fixed-pole coordinates \\(z(q)=\\exp_x(tq)\\), define\n  \\[\n  L=D_q^2\\{c_t(x,z(q))-F(z(q))\\}_{q=p}.\n  \\tag{5.5}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\nL\\geq0,\\qquad LE=0.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nFor every \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\Psi_j(d)=w_o(\\exp_x(p_j+d))+\\frac12|p_j+d|^2\n\\]\nhas lower Taylor jet \\((0,m_jL)\\) at \\(d=0\\), after subtraction of its value there. If \\(p_j\\) is nonconjugate, this gives an endpoint lower test using the chosen smooth upper cost branch.</p><p>More precisely, for \\(t&lt;s&lt;1\\), let\n\\[\nQ_s=\\frac1tA_x(tp)-\\frac1s\\sum_jm_jA_x(sp_j).\n\\]\nThen \\(Q_s\\geq0\\) on \\(E^\\perp\\), and \\(m_j(L+LQ_sL)\\) is a lower Taylor Hessian for \\(\\Psi_j\\).</p><p>If \\(G\\) is another locally \\(C^{1,1}\\) function and\n\\[\nH=D_q^2\\{c_t(x,z(q))+G(z(q))\\}_{q=p},\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nH-L=D_q^2\\{(F+G)(z(q))\\}_{q=p}.\n\\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nAt a twice differentiable maximum, \\(H\\leq L\\). At Jensen points whose gradient and upper-Hessian errors tend to zero in a fixed chart,\n\\[\nH\\leq L+\\eta\\mathrm{Id},\\qquad\\eta\\to0.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]\nFor every linear \\(U\\) with image in \\(E\\), the endpoint lower Hessian \\(H_i=m_iL\\) satisfies\n\\[\nH_i\\geq m_i(\\mathrm{Id}+U)^*H(\\mathrm{Id}+U)\n-m_i\\eta(\\mathrm{Id}+U)^*(\\mathrm{Id}+U).\n\\]\nThe endpoint jet has exactly zero linear coefficient.</p>", "proof": "<p>In normal coordinates \\(x'=\\exp_xa\\), put\n\\[\nB(a,q)=c_t(\\exp_xa,z(q)),\\quad\nf(q)=F(z(q)),\\quad\na(q)=\\log_xx(z(q)).\n\\]\nAt the reference point \\(a(p)=0\\), first variation gives\n\\[\nB(0,q)=\\frac t2|q|^2,\\qquad B_a(0,q)=-q.\n\\]\nThus \\(B_{qq}=t\\mathrm{Id}\\), \\(B_{qa}=-\\mathrm{Id}\\). The envelope identity and differentiability of the pole imply\n\\[\nD^2f=t\\mathrm{Id}-R,\\qquad R=D_qa(p)=L.\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nHence \\(R\\) is symmetric. Since \\(f(q)\\leq g(x)+B(0,q)\\), with equality at \\(p\\), \\(L\\geq0\\).</p><p>Choose \\(s\\in(t,1)\\), and define\n\\[\ny_j(d)=\\exp_x(p_j+d),\\qquad\nz_j(d)=\\exp_x(s(p_j+d)),\n\\]\n\\[\nC_j(a,d)\n=\\frac{c(\\exp_xa,z_j(d))}{s}\n+\\frac{c(z_j(d),y_j(d))}{1-s}.\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThe pieces are smooth near \\((0,0)\\), as proper subsegments of a minimizing ray. Energy splitting gives \\(c(\\exp_xa,y_j(d))\\leq C_j(a,d)\\). At \\(a=0\\),\n\\[\nC_j(0,d)=\\frac12|p_j+d|^2,\\qquad\n(C_j)_a(0,d)=-(p_j+d),\n\\]\n\\[\n(C_j)_{ad}=-\\mathrm{Id},\\qquad\n(C_j)_{aa}=\\frac1sA_x(sp_j).\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nThe fixed reference pole in \\(z_j(d)\\) makes the mixed derivative exactly \\(-\\mathrm{Id}\\).</p><p>The nonnegative function\n\\[\ng(x(z(q)))+C_j(a(q),0)+w_o(y_j)\n\\]\nvanishes at \\(p\\). It is differentiable there because\n\\(g(x(z(q)))=f(q)-B(a(q),q)\\), and its derivative is\n\\((p-p_j)^*R\\). This derivative is zero. Symmetry gives \\(LE=0\\).</p><p>On \\(E^\\perp\\), transverse MTW concavity and divided-action monotonicity give\n\\[\nQ_s\\geq\\frac1tA_x(tp)-\\frac1sA_x(sp)&gt;0.\n\\]\nAll contracted points are before cut by Proposition 1.</p><p>Fix \\(i\\), put \\(m=m_i\\), choose any linear \\(U:T_xM\\to E\\), and set\n\\[\nk=m(d+Ud),\\qquad a=a(p+k).\n\\]\nIn the lower bound defining \\(g(\\exp_xa)\\), move only endpoint \\(i\\) to \\(y_i(d)\\), replace costs by (5.8), and average with the old weights. With \\(d_i=d\\), \\(d_j=0\\) for \\(j\\neq i\\),\n\\[\nf(p+k)\\geq B(a,p+k)\n-\\sum_jm_j\\{C_j(a,d_j)+w_o(y_j(d_j))\\}.\n\\]\nSubtract the equality at zero. The linear pole terms cancel by\n\\(\\sum_jm_jp_j=p\\), and prefix terms cancel since \\(Df(p)=tp\\). Writing\n\\(\\widehat\\Psi_i(d)=\\Psi_i(d)-\\Psi_i(0)\\), the remaining inequality is\n\\[\nm\\widehat\\Psi_i(d)\n\\geq\\frac12L(k,k)+\\frac12Q_s(a,a)\n+a\\cdot(md-k)+o(|d|^2).\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nHere \\(a=Lk+o(|d|)\\). Since \\(md-k=-mUd\\) and \\(\\operatorname{range}L\\subset E^\\perp\\), the mixed term is \\(o(|d|^2)\\). The lower Hessian is\n\\[\nm(\\mathrm{Id}+U)^*(L+LQ_sL)(\\mathrm{Id}+U)\n=m(L+LQ_sL)\\geq mL,\n\\]\nbecause \\(LU=0\\). This proves the exact zero-linear-part jet.</p><p>The map \\(d\\mapsto y_i(d)\\) need not be invertible for this calculation. When \\(p_i\\) is nonconjugate, it is a local diffeomorphism and \\(|p_i+d|^2/2\\) is the chosen smooth upper branch of \\(c(x,y_i(d))\\), giving the endpoint jet.</p><p>Equation (5.12) is the difference of the definitions. At a maximum it yields \\(H\\leq L\\). At Jensen points the coordinate-change term involves the gradient error and tends to zero, giving (5.13). Since \\((\\mathrm{Id}+U)^*L(\\mathrm{Id}+U)=L\\), the final displayed endpoint inequality follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Center semibound before the center Jacobian — original Lemma 5.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Center semibound before the center Jacobian — original Lemma 5.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one original potential \\(v\\), one set of selected scales, and the templates of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\varphi_c(h)=h+bB_c((h-a)/D),\\qquad\n  \\varphi_o(h)=h+bB_o((h-a)/D),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  w_c=\\varphi_c\\circ v,\\qquad w_o=\\varphi_o\\circ v.\n  \\]\n  At sufficiently small scales both scalar derivatives lie between \\(1/2\\) and \\(2\\). On the fixed compact center-level interval, \\(B_c'&lt;0\\) is bounded away from zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t_j\\uparrow1\\), \\(b_j\\in[b_-,b_+]\\), and\n  \\[\n  F_j=Q_{t_j}g_{b_j},\\qquad G_j=Q_{1-t_j}w_{c,b_j}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z_j\\) be simultaneous differentiability points for Proposition 9 and second-order expansion points for \\(G_j\\), with center levels in the stated compact interval, and suppose\n  \\[\n  |\\operatorname{grad}(F_j+G_j)(z_j)|\\to0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x_j\\) be the prefix pole, \\(z_j=\\exp_{x_j}(t_jp_j)\\), and \\(H_j\\) the matrix (5.11).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(c,C&gt;0\\), depending on the geometry and fixed center template but not on \\(K\\), such that\n\\[\nH_j\\geq c\\frac{b_j}{D}\\mathrm{Id}\n-C\\frac{b_j}{D^2}p_jp_j^*\n-\\epsilon_j\\mathrm{Id},\n\\qquad\\epsilon_j\\to0.\n\\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nThe errors tend to zero with prefix and gradient errors at fixed scales. No upper bound on \\(H_j\\), and no Alexandrov Hessian of \\(v\\) at the center, is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(\\tau_j=1-t_j\\), let \\(y_j\\) be the unique final minimizer, and set\n\\[\nr_j=\\tau_j^{-1}\\log_{y_j}z_j,\\qquad\nl_j=\\varphi_c'(v(y_j))&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe short minimum gives a smooth lower test for \\(w_c\\) with gradient \\(r_j\\). Inverting \\(\\varphi_c\\) gives a lower test for \\(v\\) with gradient \\(r_j/l_j\\). Proposition 1 therefore makes\n\\[\n\\zeta_j=\\exp_{y_j}(r_j/l_j)\n\\]\na true contact endpoint on the indicated minimizing ray. The point\n\\(\\widehat x_j=\\exp_{y_j}r_j\\) is strictly before cut.</p><p>Omit subscripts. Put\n\\[\n\\theta=(1+l^{-1})/2,\\qquad \\xi=\\exp_y(\\theta r).\n\\]\nSplit the true support at fraction \\(l\\theta\\):\n\\[\nC(h)=\\frac{c(h,\\xi)}{l\\theta}\n+\\frac{c(\\xi,\\zeta)}{1-l\\theta}.\n\\]\nIt equals \\(c(y,\\zeta)\\) at \\(y\\), and\n\\[\nv(h)\\geq v(y)+c(y,\\zeta)-C(h).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\psi(h)=\\varphi_c\\{v(y)+c(y,\\zeta)-C(h)\\}\n\\]\nis a smooth lower support for \\(w_c\\), with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{grad}\\psi(y)=r,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Hess}\\psi(y)\n=-\\theta^{-1}A_y(\\theta r)\n+\\frac{\\varphi_c''(v(y))}{l^2}rr^*.\n\\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nThe true endpoint may be conjugate, but \\(\\xi\\) is halfway into the available extension and is before cut.</p><p>At fixed scales \\(1-l\\) is bounded below positively, and the parameters range over a compact set. The supports \\(\\psi\\) have uniform smooth bounds and a common supporting radius. All minimizers of \\(Q_\\tau w_c(z)\\) lie within \\(O(\\tau)\\) of \\(z\\). For small \\(\\tau\\), the local \\(Q_\\tau\\psi\\) is smooth, has minimizer \\(y\\), and supports \\(Q_\\tau w_c\\) from below. The minimizing Hessian is \\(\\tau^{-1}\\mathrm{Id}+O(1)&gt;0\\); the implicit-function theorem and Schur formula give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hess}Q_\\tau\\psi(z)\n=\\operatorname{Hess}\\psi(y)+O(\\tau)\n\\tag{5.16}\n\\]\nafter parallel identification.</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\widehat p=\\log_{\\widehat x}y,\\qquad\n\\widehat L=(d\\exp_{\\widehat x})_{\\widehat p}.\n\\]\nProposition 2 applied through duration \\(1/l\\) gives\n\\[\n\\widehat L^*\n\\{A_y(r)-\\theta^{-1}A_y(\\theta r)\\}\n\\widehat L\\geq c(1-l)\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\]\nGauss's lemma gives \\(\\widehat L^*r=-\\widehat p\\), hence\n\\[\n\\widehat L^*\\{\\operatorname{Hess}\\psi(y)+A_y(r)\\}\\widehat L\n\\geq c(1-l)\\mathrm{Id}\n+\\frac{\\varphi_c''(v(y))}{l^2}\\widehat p\\widehat p^*.\n\\tag{5.17}\n\\]\nHere \\(1-l\\geq cb/D\\) and \\(|\\varphi_c''|/l^2\\leq Cb/D^2\\).</p><p>The envelope identities are\n\\[\nx_j=\\exp_{z_j}(-t_j\\operatorname{grad}F_j(z_j)),\\qquad\n\\widehat x_j=\\exp_{z_j}(t_j\\operatorname{grad}G_j(z_j)).\n\\]\nTheir distance tends to zero. The rays through \\(\\widehat x_j\\) have a uniform fixed-scale extension, so relevant cost and inverse exponential charts are uniformly smooth.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nU=Q_\\tau\\psi,\\quad Z(q)=\\exp_x(tq),\\quad\nM=D_qZ(p),\\quad R=\\nabla_q^2Z(p).\n\\]\nSince \\(G-U\\) has a local minimum at \\(z\\),\n\\[\nH\\geq H_\\psi\n:=t\\mathrm{Id}+M^*\\operatorname{Hess}U(z)M\n+\\langle\\operatorname{grad}U(z),R\\rangle.\n\\]\nOnly bounded derivatives of the smooth support occur on the right. If\n\\(\\delta=|\\operatorname{grad}(F+G)(z)|\\), then\n\\(d(x,\\widehat x)=O(\\delta)\\), and \\(p,\\widehat p\\), after parallel identification, differ by \\(O_{\\mathrm{fixed}}(\\delta)\\). Replacing \\(x,p\\) by \\(\\widehat x,\\widehat p\\), then \\(t\\) by one, changes the expression by \\(O_{\\mathrm{fixed}}(\\tau+\\delta)\\).</p><p>For \\(Y(q)=\\exp_{\\widehat x}q\\), \\(B=DY(\\widehat p)\\), the branch identity\n\\(c(\\widehat x,Y(q))=|q|^2/2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Id}\n=B^*A_y(r)B-\\langle r,\\nabla_q^2Y(\\widehat p)\\rangle.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\operatorname{grad}\\psi(y)=r\\) and (5.16),\n\\[\nH\\geq H_\\psi\n=B^*\\{\\operatorname{Hess}\\psi(y)+A_y(r)\\}B\n+O_{\\mathrm{fixed}}(\\tau+\\delta).\n\\]\nCombine with (5.17). No coordinate error multiplies the unknown Hessian of \\(G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Undoing the outer modification — original Lemma 5.6.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Undoing the outer modification — original Lemma 5.6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(v\\) be an original potential for an admissible density pair.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(b&gt;0\\), \\(w_o=\\varphi_o\\circ v\\), \\(g_b=w_o^c\\), and an active pair\n  \\[\n  g_b(x)+c(x,y)+\\varphi_o(v(y))=0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The scalar map \\(\\varphi_o\\) is smooth and increasing, and at \\(v(y)\\),\n  \\[\n  l=\\varphi_o'(v(y))&gt;1,\\qquad\n  \\varphi_o''(v(y))&lt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a minimizing reverse log \\(r\\) from \\(y\\) to \\(x\\), and let \\(p\\) be its corresponding forward log.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_o\\geq0\\) be a lower Taylor Hessian, with zero linear part, for\n  \\[\n  d\\longmapsto w_o(\\exp_x(p+d))+\\frac12|p+d|^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The specified modified ray is nonconjugate, and \\(r/l\\) is before cut. With \\(B=(d\\exp_x)_p\\), define\n\\[\nV_o\n=H_o+B^*\\{lA_y(r/l)-A_y(r)\\}B\n-\\frac{\\varphi_o''(v(y))}{l^2}pp^*.\n\\tag{5.19}\n\\]\nThen \\(V_o&gt;0\\) and\n\\[\n\\det V_o\n\\leq l^n(\\Lambda/\\lambda)\n\\frac{\\sigma_x(p)^2}{\\sigma_y(r/l)}.\n\\tag{5.20}\n\\]\nThe middle term in (5.19) is positive definite.</p><p>On a compact family of such active pairs, with \\(b\\) bounded away from zero and the slope and curvature inequalities holding with fixed strict margins, this middle term has a uniform positive definite baseline and the specified exponential branches are uniformly nonsingular.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 11.1 makes the endpoint coordinate map locally invertible. Its chosen smooth cost branch has endpoint Hessian \\(A_y(r)\\). The zero linear part fixes the gradient of the induced lower test for \\(w_o\\) to be \\(r\\).</p><p>Invert \\(\\varphi_o\\) and use the scalar chain rule. Formula (5.19) is \\(l\\) times the true relative lower-test Hessian, pulled back by \\(B\\). Gauss's lemma gives the displayed rank-one term. Divided action between times \\(1/l\\) and one decreases strictly, so the middle term is positive definite and \\(V_o&gt;0\\).</p><p>Decrease the lower Taylor Hessian by \\(\\epsilon\\mathrm{Id}\\), apply Proposition 8 to the resulting genuine test, and let \\(\\epsilon\\downarrow0\\). Determinants of scaling and pullback give (5.20).</p><p>For uniformity, include the chosen minimizing log among the parameters. Activity is closed on the compact family. Lemma 11.1 excludes a conjugate limit, and radial shortening excludes a true cut limit. Compactness gives uniform branch bounds and a positive divided-action baseline. Multiple ordinary branches cause no difficulty, since each specified nonconjugate minimizing branch is a smooth upper branch included in the parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Conjugacy excluded by an active increasing modification — original Lemma 5.5.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Conjugacy excluded by an active increasing modification — original Lemma 5.5.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the active pair, scalar modification, and chosen minimizing reverse log of Proposition 11.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The modified ray is nonconjugate at its full endpoint, and its shortened true log \\(r/l\\) is before cut.</p>", "proof": "<p>The active inequality is\n\\[\n\\varphi_o(v(h))\\geq-c(x,h)-g_b(x).\n\\]\nFor \\(1/l&lt;s&lt;1\\), put \\(\\xi_s=\\exp_y(sr)\\), and form the smooth upper support\n\\[\nC_s(h)=c(h,\\xi_s)/s+c(\\xi_s,x)/(1-s).\n\\]\nIts value at \\(y\\) is \\(c(y,x)\\), and its gradient is \\(-r\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\varphi_o^{-1}(-C_s-g_b(x))\n\\]\nis a smooth lower test for \\(v\\), with gradient \\(r/l\\). This shortened vector is strictly before cut, independently of any density equation. Proposition 1 also gives a global true contact.</p><p>The scaled true relative Hessian is\n\\[\nW_s=lA_y(r/l)-s^{-1}A_y(sr)\n-\\frac{\\varphi_o''(v(y))}{l^2}rr^*.\n\\tag{5.18}\n\\]\nFix \\(s_0\\in(1/l,1)\\). Divided-action monotonicity gives a positive definite baseline for the first two terms for every \\(s\\geq s_0\\); the last term is positive semidefinite.</p><p>If the duration-one endpoint were conjugate, the index-form characterization, equivalently Lemma 2.2, would make an eigenvalue of \\(s^{-1}A_y(sr)\\) tend to \\(-\\infty\\). Hence \\(\\det W_s\\to\\infty\\). Proposition 8 instead gives\n\\[\n\\det W_s\n\\leq l^n(\\Lambda/\\lambda)/\\sigma_y(r/l)&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\na contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Limiting matrix configuration — original Proposition 5.8.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Limiting matrix configuration — original Proposition 5.8.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(F\\) has no power upper bound, and use the scales of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the center template and all constants in Propositions 5–7, in particular\n  \\[\n  m_*=\\frac{c_1}{12(n+1)},\\qquad M_0=2/m_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(K\\), then an outer template whose curvature is less than \\(-K\\) in the prescribed capped region, and only then take selected scales sufficiently small.</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  \\varphi_c(h)=h+bB_c((h-a)/D),\\qquad\n  \\varphi_o(h)=h+bB_o((h-a)/D).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently small selected scale there are \\(b\\in[b_-,b_+]\\), a pole \\(x\\), logs \\(p,p_j\\in G_x\\), positive weights \\(m_j\\) summing to one, and symmetric forms \\(H,L,V,V_i\\) on \\(T_xM\\), with a selected index \\(i\\), such that\n\\[\np=\\sum_jm_jp_j,\\qquad\nE=\\operatorname{span}\\{p_j-p\\}=\\mathbb Re,\n\\]\n\\[\n|e|=1,\\qquad q=p\\cdot e&gt;0,\\qquad q^2\\geq cD,\n\\tag{5.21}\n\\]\n\\[\nm_i\\geq m_*&gt;0,\\qquad\np_i=p+d_ie,\\quad d_i&gt;0,\n\\tag{5.22}\n\\]\n\\[\nL\\geq0,\\qquad LE=0,\\qquad H\\leq L,\n\\]\n\\[\nH\\geq V+c\\frac bD\\mathrm{Id}\n-C\\frac b{D^2}pp^*,\\qquad V&gt;0,\n\\tag{5.23}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nV_i\\geq m_iL+cK\\frac b{D^2}p_ip_i^*,\\qquad V_i&gt;0.\n\\tag{5.24}\n\\]\nThe center and selected outer modified rays are nonconjugate.</p><p>Let \\(y=\\exp_xp\\), \\(y_i=\\exp_xp_i\\), let \\(r,r_i\\) be their specified reverse logs, and set\n\\[\nl=\\varphi_c'(v(y)),\\qquad l_i=\\varphi_o'(v(y_i)).\n\\]\nThe specified limiting true branches are nonconjugate, and\n\\[\n\\det V\\geq c\\frac{\\sigma_x(p)^2}{\\sigma_y(r/l)},\n\\qquad\n\\det V_i\\leq C\\frac{\\sigma_x(p_i)^2}{\\sigma_{y_i}(r_i/l_i)}.\n\\tag{5.25}\n\\]\nThe level, weight, moment, and norm-increment conclusions of Proposition 7 also hold.</p><p>The constants \\(c,C,m_*\\) in these assertions are independent of \\(K\\) and the outer template. No lower Taylor jet or Alexandrov Hessian is asserted at the limiting endpoints.</p>", "proof": "<p>**Step 1: fixed scales and exact maxima.**  \nFix the original potential, scales, and both templates throughout this proof. Constants used only to pass limits may depend on them. Choose \\(t_k\\uparrow1\\) and interior maxima \\((z_k^0,b_k)\\) from Proposition 5 for\n\\[\nF_k=Q_{t_k}g_{b_k},\\qquad\nG_k=Q_{1-t_k}w_{c,b_k}.\n\\]\nThese functions are spatially \\(C^{1,1}\\) by Proposition 1. At an exact maximum, the velocities align. If \\(x_k^0,y_k^0\\) are the minimizers and\n\\[\np_k^0=t_k^{-1}\\log_{x_k^0}z_k^0,\n\\]\nthen \\(y_k^0=\\exp_{x_k^0}p_k^0\\) along the concatenated minimizing ray. Proposition 6 applies to every positive active representation of \\(p_k^0\\), including its level and common-section assertions.</p><p>**Step 2: remove null sets before Jensen sampling.**  \nLet \\(N\\) be the original-potential exceptional set of Proposition 8. For fixed \\(k\\), the final minimizer map \\(Y_k\\) is locally Lipschitz, hence differentiable almost everywhere. The area formula makes\n\\[\nZ_k=\n\\{z:DY_k(z)\\text{ exists},\\ \\det DY_k(z)\\neq0,\\ Y_k(z)\\in N\\}\n\\tag{5.26}\n\\]\na null set: the integral of \\(|\\det DY_k|\\) over \\(Y_k^{-1}(N)\\) is zero. This requires no advance knowledge of nonsingularity.</p><p>Apply the semiconvex maximum lemma [3, Appendix, Lemmas A.3–A.4] to \\(F_k+G_k\\) at \\(z_k^0\\), using a small fourth-order strictness penalty if needed. Its positive-measure conclusion permits points \\(z_{k,\\nu}\\to z_k^0\\), outside \\(Z_k\\) and all derivative exceptional sets for \\(F_k,G_k\\), the prefix pole, and \\(Y_k\\), with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{grad}(F_k+G_k)(z_{k,\\nu})\\to0,\\qquad\nD^2(F_k+G_k)(z_{k,\\nu})\\leq\\eta_{k,\\nu}\\mathrm{Id},\n\\quad\\eta_{k,\\nu}\\to0.\n\\tag{5.27}\n\\]\nChoose active representations with at most \\(n+1\\) vertices, padding by zero weights only for compactness. Proposition 9 gives exact matrices \\(L_{k,\\nu}\\geq0\\), annihilation of active differences, and endpoint lower jets \\(m_{i,k,\\nu}L_{k,\\nu}\\). Its comparison gives \\(H_{k,\\nu}\\leq L_{k,\\nu}+o(1)\\mathrm{Id}\\).</p><p>**Step 3: first take fixed-prefix limits.**  \nFor fixed \\(k\\), local \\(C^{1,1}\\) bounds are finite. Extract a subsequence in \\(\\nu\\) so that configurations, weights, \\(H_{k,\\nu},L_{k,\\nu}\\) converge. Discard zero limiting weights. The limiting endpoints are active at the exact maximum, and their weighted sum is \\(p_k\\). Thus the universal parameter inequality applies to this representation, without asserting persistence of a prescribed vertex. Write\n\\[\nL_k\\geq0,\\qquad L_kE_k=0,\\qquad H_k\\leq L_k.\n\\tag{5.28}\n\\]\nThe quantitative error in Proposition 10 is bounded by a fixed-scale constant times\n\\(1-t_k+|\\operatorname{grad}(F_k+G_k)|\\). After \\(\\nu\\to\\infty\\), and for large \\(k\\),\n\\[\nH_k\\geq c\\frac{b_k}{D}\\mathrm{Id}\n-C\\frac{b_k}{D^2}p_kp_k^*.\n\\tag{5.29}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\dim E_k\\geq2\\), a nonzero vector in \\(E_k\\cap p_k^\\perp\\) contradicts (5.28)–(5.29). If \\(E_k=0\\), all represented logs and endpoints equal the center; \\(B_c\\leq B_o\\) at every level then contradicts the strict parameter inequality. Hence\n\\[\nE_k=\\mathbb Re_k.\n\\]\nOrient \\(e_k\\) so \\(q_k=p_k\\cdot e_k&gt;0\\); (5.28)–(5.29) give \\(q_k^2\\geq cD\\).</p><p>Proposition 7 supplies an outward index with weight at least \\(m_*\\), capped outer value, and a fixed share of outward moment. Its displacement has a positive lower bound at these fixed scales. Relabel this index as \\(1\\).</p><p>**Step 4: fix the outer branch before bounding the center Hessian.**  \nExtract a subsequence in \\(k\\) so the selected configurations and weights converge. The selected limiting weight is at least \\(m_*\\), the displacement is strictly outward, and the template value is below the prescribed cap. Impose the template conditions under a slightly larger fixed cap, so the limiting slope and curvature have strict margins:\n\\[\nB_o'&gt;0,\\qquad B_o''&lt;-K.\n\\]\nThe limiting pair is active. Proposition 11 excludes conjugacy of its modified branch using only activity and the original contact-volume bound; the shortened true log is before cut. It gives a neighborhood with a divided-action baseline \\(\\delta\\mathrm{Id}\\), \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), and determinant upper bound \\(M&lt;\\infty\\). These constants may depend on the fixed scales and templates.</p><p>Return, for each remaining \\(k\\), to its Jensen subsequence and choose \\(\\nu(k)\\) so large that configurations and matrices are within \\(1/k\\) of the fixed-prefix limits, the selected weight is at least \\(m_*/2\\), and the selected endpoint lies in this neighborhood. Require also the errors in (5.27), including their transformed versions, to be at most \\((1-t_k)^3/k\\). These requirements are compatible because \\(k\\) is fixed during the \\(\\nu\\)-limit. From now on, subscripts \\(k\\) denote this diagonal sequence of actual Jensen points.</p><p>**Step 5: use the outer test to bound the exact pole matrix.**  \nAt each diagonal point, Proposition 9 gives the actual endpoint lower Hessian\n\\[\nH_{o,k}=m_{1,k}L_k\\geq0.\n\\]\nUndo the modification with Proposition 11 and call the resulting form \\(V_{1,k}\\). The curvature term is positive and the divided-action baseline gives\n\\[\nV_{1,k}\\geq m_{1,k}L_k+\\delta\\mathrm{Id},\n\\qquad\n\\det V_{1,k}\\leq M.\n\\tag{5.30}\n\\]\nEvery eigenvalue is at least \\(\\delta\\), so the largest is at most \\(M\\delta^{1-n}\\). Since \\(m_{1,k}\\geq m_*/2\\), \\(L_k\\) is bounded above. Proposition 10 and \\(H_k\\leq L_k+o(1)\\mathrm{Id}\\) bound \\(H_k\\) on both sides. No center density equation has been used.</p><p>The center extension supplies uniformly nonsingular fixed-scale prefix-log coordinates, as in Proposition 10. Their first two derivatives and \\(\\operatorname{grad}G_k\\) are bounded. The coordinate definition of \\(H_k\\) therefore yields\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{Hess}G_k(z_k)\\|\\leq C_{\\mathrm{fixed}}.\n\\tag{5.31}\n\\]</p><p>**Step 6: establish that sampled centers are good original endpoints.**  \nPut\n\\[\n\\tau_k=1-t_k,\\qquad y_k=Y_k(z_k),\\qquad\nK_k(z,y)=c(z,y)/\\tau_k.\n\\]\nThe envelope identity gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hess}G_k\n=(K_k)_{zz}+(K_k)_{zy}DY_k.\n\\]\nSince \\(d(z_k,y_k)=O(\\tau_k)\\), the short-cost expansions in parallel frames are\n\\[\n(K_k)_{zz}=\\tau_k^{-1}\\mathrm{Id}+O(\\tau_k),\\quad\n(K_k)_{yy}=\\tau_k^{-1}\\mathrm{Id}+O(\\tau_k),\n\\]\n\\[\n(K_k)_{zy}=-\\tau_k^{-1}\\Pi+O(\\tau_k).\n\\]\nSolving with (5.31),\n\\[\nDY_k\n=\\Pi^{-1}\\{\\mathrm{Id}-\\tau_k\\operatorname{Hess}G_k\\}\n+O(\\tau_k^2).\n\\tag{5.32}\n\\]\nIt is nonsingular for large \\(k\\). Since \\(z_k\\notin Z_k\\), one has \\(y_k\\notin N\\), justifying the original Alexandrov and Jacobian statements at the centers.</p><p>At \\(y_k\\), \\(w_c=\\varphi_c\\circ v\\) has an Alexandrov Hessian. For every nearby \\(z\\), minimizing supplies a proximal subgradient\n\\[\n\\xi(z)=-(K_k)_y(z,Y_k(z))\\in\\partial w_c(Y_k(z)).\n\\]\nLemma 12.1, Lipschitz dependence, and differentiability of \\(Y_k\\) give\n\\[\n\\xi(z_k+h)\n=\\operatorname{grad}w_c(y_k)\n+\\operatorname{Hess}w_c(y_k)DY_k(z_k)h+o(|h|).\n\\]\nExpanding the smooth cost derivative,\n\\[\n\\{\\operatorname{Hess}w_c(y_k)+(K_k)_{yy}\\}DY_k\n+(K_k)_{yz}=0.\n\\]\nSolving with (5.32) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hess}w_c(y_k)\n=\\operatorname{Hess}G_k(z_k)+O(\\tau_k).\n\\tag{5.33}\n\\]\nThis bounds the endpoint Hessians without assuming their bound in advance, and does not require neighboring endpoints to be smooth.</p><p>**Step 7: insert the true center form and take matrix limits.**  \nSet\n\\[\nr_k=\\tau_k^{-1}\\log_{y_k}z_k,\\qquad\nl_k=\\varphi_c'(v(y_k)),\\qquad\n\\widehat x_k=\\exp_{y_k}r_k,\\qquad\n\\widehat p_k=\\log_{\\widehat x_k}y_k.\n\\]\nThe true endpoint \\(\\zeta_k=\\exp_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)\\) is before cut because \\(y_k\\notin N\\). Identify \\(T_{x_k}M\\) with \\(T_{\\widehat x_k}M\\) by parallel transport, and let \\(B_k\\) be the exponential differential at \\(\\widehat p_k\\) after that identification. Define\n\\[\nV_k\n=l_kB_k^*\\{\\operatorname{Hess}v(y_k)+A_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)\\}B_k&gt;0.\n\\tag{5.34}\n\\]\nThe exact scalar chain rule yields\n\\[\nB_k^*\\{\\operatorname{Hess}w_c(y_k)+A_{y_k}(r_k)\\}B_k\n=V_k\n+B_k^*\\{A_{y_k}(r_k)-l_kA_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)\\}B_k\n+\\frac{\\varphi_c''(v(y_k))}{l_k^2}\n \\widehat p_k\\widehat p_k^*.\n\\tag{5.35}\n\\]\nProposition 2 bounds the middle term below by \\(c(1-l_k)\\mathrm{Id}\\). Equation (5.33) and the vanishing pole mismatch identify the left side with \\(H_k+o(1)\\mathrm{Id}\\), by the same smooth-coordinate comparison as Proposition 10, now using bounded unregularized Hessians. Thus\n\\[\nH_k\\geq V_k+c\\frac{b_k}{D}\\mathrm{Id}\n-C\\frac{b_k}{D^2}p_kp_k^*-o(1)\\mathrm{Id}.\n\\]\nThis also bounds \\(V_k\\) above.</p><p>Taking determinants in (5.34) and applying (5.4),\n\\[\n\\det V_k\n=l_k^n\\sigma_{\\widehat x_k}(\\widehat p_k)^2\n\\frac{|\\det D(\\exp_{\\cdot}\\operatorname{grad}v)(y_k)|}\n     {\\sigma_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)}\n\\geq\nc\\frac{\\sigma_{\\widehat x_k}(\\widehat p_k)^2}\n        {\\sigma_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)}.\n\\tag{5.36}\n\\]\nScalar derivatives lie between \\(1/2\\) and \\(2\\), so these density constants are independent of \\(K\\).</p><p>The center modified rays have a uniform fixed-scale extension:\n\\(1-l_k\\geq cb_-/D&gt;0\\). Compactness of proper minimizing subsegments gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_{\\widehat x_k}(\\widehat p_k)\\geq c_{\\mathrm{fixed}}&gt;0.\n\\]\nSince \\(0&lt;V_k\\leq C_{\\mathrm{fixed}}\\mathrm{Id}\\), (5.36) implies\n\\[\n\\sigma_{y_k}(r_k/l_k)\n\\geq\n\\frac{c\\,\\sigma_{\\widehat x_k}(\\widehat p_k)^2}{\\det V_k}\n\\geq c'_{\\mathrm{fixed}}&gt;0.\n\\]\nContinuity excludes a conjugate true-center limit. An ordinary cut limit is allowed, using its specified nonsingular branch.</p><p>Extract a final subsequence for \\(b_k,H_k,L_k,V_k,V_{1,k}\\) and configurations. Discard additional zero limiting weights. The selected weight remains at least \\(m_*\\), and its displacement retains its positive fixed-scale lower bound. The barycenter identity leaves another surviving vertex, so the surviving difference span remains a nonzero line. The outer modified and true branches are uniformly nonsingular from Step 4; the center modified branch has its fixed-scale extension. Equation (5.36) yields a positive limiting determinant, so \\(V&gt;0\\).</p><p>The limits of Proposition 9 and (5.35) give (5.23). At selected outer endpoints, (5.19) has positive divided-action term and\n\\[\n-\\frac{\\varphi_o''(v(y_{1,k}))}{l_{1,k}^2}\n=\n-\\frac{b_kB_o''((v(y_{1,k})-a)/D)}\n       {D^2l_{1,k}^2}\n\\geq cK b_k/D^2.\n\\]\nPass this inequality and (5.20) to the limit to obtain (5.24) and the outer inequality in (5.25). All level and configuration estimates pass along the same subsequence. Their constants and cap were fixed before \\(K\\); only the auxiliary baseline, determinant bound, limiting rates, and Jensen radii depended on the fixed outer template.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Subgradients at an Alexandrov point — original Lemma 5.7.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Subgradients at an Alexandrov point — original Lemma 5.7.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(f\\) be semiconvex near zero and have the Alexandrov expansion\n\\[\nf(h)=f(0)+p\\cdot h+\\frac12A(h,h)+o(|h|^2).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nearby subgradient satisfies\n\\[\n\\zeta=p+Ah+o(|h|),\n\\qquad \\zeta\\in\\partial f(h),\n\\]\nuniformly over the choice of \\(\\zeta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Add a fixed quadratic to reduce to the convex case. Write the remainder \\(R(h)\\) with\n\\[\n|R(h)|\\leq\\omega(|h|)|h|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\omega\\) is nondecreasing and tends to zero. For \\(r=|h|&gt;0\\), a unit vector \\(e\\), and \\(0&lt;\\alpha\\leq1\\), subgradient inequalities at \\(h\\pm\\alpha re\\) give\n\\[\n|(\\zeta-p-Ah)\\cdot e|\n\\leq\\frac12\\|A\\|\\alpha r+5\\omega(2r)r/\\alpha.\n\\]\nChoose\n\\[\n\\alpha=\\sqrt{\\max\\{\\omega(2r),r^2\\}}\n\\]\nfor small \\(r\\), and take the supremum over unit \\(e\\). Subtracting the added quadratic restores the stated formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Oblique projection and the missing direction — original Lemma 5.9.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Oblique projection and the missing direction — original Lemma 5.9.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let the matrices, vectors, weights, and constants satisfy (5.21)–(5.24) of Proposition 12.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(c&gt;0\\), depending on the fixed center-template constants, dimension, and \\(m_*\\), but not on \\(K\\), such that\n\\[\n\\det V_i\\geq cK\\det V.\n\\tag{5.37}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\beta=Cb/D^2,\\qquad \\gamma=cKb/D^2,\n\\]\nincreasing \\(C\\) if necessary, and define\n\\[\nP=\\mathrm{Id}-ep^*/q.\n\\]\nThis is projection onto \\(p^\\perp\\) along \\(e\\). Since \\(LE=0\\),\n\\(P^*LP=L\\). On \\(p^\\perp\\), the center inequality gives \\(H\\geq V\\), and \\(L\\geq H\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nV_i\\geq m_iP^*VP+\\gamma p_ip_i^*.\n\\tag{5.38}\n\\]\nAlso \\(H(e,e)\\leq L(e,e)=0\\) gives \\(V(e,e)\\leq\\beta q^2\\).</p><p>Let \\(Q\\) have orthonormal columns spanning \\(p^\\perp\\). In the nonsingular basis \\([Q,e]\\), write\n\\[\n[Q,e]^*V[Q,e]\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;z\\\\z^*&amp;h\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nS=h-z^*A^{-1}z&gt;0.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nS\\leq h=V(e,e)\\leq\\beta q^2.\n\\]\nIn this basis, the determinant of the right side of (5.38) is\n\\[\nm_i^{n-1}\\det A\\,\\gamma(p_i\\cdot e)^2.\n\\]\nTaking ratios cancels the nonorthogonal basis determinant:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\det V_i}{\\det V}\n\\geq m_i^{n-1}\\frac{\\gamma(p_i\\cdot e)^2}{S}\n\\geq m_i^{n-1}\\frac{\\gamma}{\\beta}\n\\frac{(p_i\\cdot e)^2}{q^2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(p_i=p+d_ie\\), \\(d_i&gt;0\\), and \\(q&gt;0\\), \\(p_i\\cdot e\\geq q\\). Use \\(m_i\\geq m_*\\) and \\(\\gamma/\\beta\\geq cK\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Power bound for the class-wide section oscillation — original Proposition 5.10.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Power bound for the class-wide section oscillation — original Proposition 5.10.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed weak-MTW manifold and density bounds of Theorem 1, and the section-oscillation supremum \\(F\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(\\beta&gt;0\\), \\(C_0&lt;\\infty\\), and \\(r_0&gt;0\\), common to the entire density class, such that\n\\[\nF(r)\\leq C_0r^\\beta\n\\qquad(0&lt;r&lt;r_0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose otherwise, and take the scale sequence of Proposition 5. Fix the center and weight constants, then choose \\(K\\) and its outer template, and only then take scales sufficiently small for Proposition 12.</p><p>Proposition 13 and the determinant bounds (5.25) give\n\\[\ncK\\frac{\\sigma_x(p)^2}{\\sigma_y(r/l)}\n\\leq\\det V_i\n\\leq C\\frac{\\sigma_x(p_i)^2}{\\sigma_{y_i}(r_i/l_i)}.\n\\]\nThe radial comparisons of Proposition 2 give\n\\[\n\\sigma_y(r/l)\\leq C\\sigma_x(p),\\qquad\n\\sigma_{y_i}(r_i/l_i)\\geq c\\sigma_x(p_i).\n\\]\nFor the selected outward endpoint,\n\\[\nm_i\\geq m_*,\\qquad\nq^2\\geq cD,\\qquad\n|p_i|^2-|p|^2\\leq3D.\n\\]\nProposition 3 applies with \\(\\mu=m_*\\), \\(\\kappa=c\\), \\(L_0=3\\), all fixed before \\(K\\), and gives\n\\[\n\\sigma_x(p)\\geq c'\\sigma_x(p_i).\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\ncK\\sigma_x(p)\n\\leq C\\sigma_x(p_i)\n\\leq C'\\sigma_x(p).\n\\]\nThe center modified branch is nonconjugate, so \\(\\sigma_x(p)&gt;0\\). This bounds \\(K\\) by a constant fixed before its choice. Choosing \\(K\\) larger is a contradiction.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 2, "cite"], [1, 5, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 12, "cite"], [1, 13, "cite"], [1, 15, "cite"], [1, 17, "cite"], [1, 18, "cite"], [1, 20, "cite"], [1, 21, "cite"], [2, 5, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 21, "cite"], [2, 24, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [4, 1, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 6, "cite"], [4, 20, "cite"], [5, 24, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 8, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 24, "cite"], [8, 13, "cite"], [8, 14, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [8, 24, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 8, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [11, 17, "cite"], [12, 8, "cite"], [12, 18, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 21, "cite"], [14, 21, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [15, 21, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 21, "cite"], [19, 21, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [21, 24, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 0, "cite"]]});
